/* Cross-domain integration scenarios */
window.INTEGRATION = [
 { id:"ix01", title:"Disk full, but du disagrees", level:2, domains:["p3","p4","p26"],
   context:"You look after files01, a small Ubuntu server where a Python service (uploader.py) receives job photos from the crew's tablets and saves them under /srv/uploads. This morning every tablet shows 'upload failed'. Yesterday a coworker noticed the root disk filling up and deleted /var/log/uploader/debug.log (about 36 GB), but the disk-space alert never cleared.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"journalctl -u uploader --since today (excerpt)", text:"Oct 26 06:58:12 files01 python3[1184]: ERROR upload failed for tablet-07\nOct 26 06:58:12 files01 python3[1184]: Traceback (most recent call last):\nOct 26 06:58:12 files01 python3[1184]:   File \"/opt/uploader/uploader.py\", line 88, in save_chunk\nOct 26 06:58:12 files01 python3[1184]:     out.write(chunk)\nOct 26 06:58:12 files01 python3[1184]: OSError: [Errno 28] No space left on device\nOct 26 07:03:40 files01 python3[1184]: ERROR upload failed for tablet-03\nOct 26 07:03:40 files01 python3[1184]: OSError: [Errno 28] No space left on device"},
     {label:"df, du and lsof +L1", text:"$ df -h /\nFilesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\n/dev/sda2        49G   47G     0 100% /\n\n$ df -i /\nFilesystem      Inodes  IUsed   IFree IUse% Mounted on\n/dev/sda2      3276800 141022 3135778    5% /\n\n$ sudo du -xsh /\n11G\t/\n\n$ sudo du -xh --max-depth=1 /var | sort -h | tail -3\n410M\t/var/lib\n1.1G\t/var/log\n1.7G\t/var\n\n$ sudo lsof +L1\nCOMMAND  PID   USER   FD   TYPE DEVICE    SIZE/OFF NLINK   NODE NAME\npython3 1184 upload    3w   REG    8,2 38654705664     0 262153 /var/log/uploader/debug.log (deleted)"},
     {label:"uploader.py (logging setup and upload loop, excerpt)", text:"import logging\nlogging.basicConfig(\n    filename=\"/var/log/uploader/debug.log\",\n    level=logging.DEBUG,\n    format=\"%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(message)s\",\n)\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"uploader\")\n...\nfor n, chunk in enumerate(iter(lambda: req.read(65536), b\"\")):\n    log.debug(\"chunk %d of %s: %r\", n, name, chunk[:64])\n    out.write(chunk)"},
     {label:"Coworker's note in the ticket, plus SMART check", text:"Note: 'Disk is 4 years old - maybe it is dying and reporting the wrong size?'\n\n$ sudo smartctl -H -A /dev/sda | egrep 'overall|Reallocated|Pending|Uncorrect'\nSMART overall-health self-assessment test result: PASSED\n  5 Reallocated_Sector_Ct   0x0033   100   100   010    Pre-fail  Always       -       0\n197 Current_Pending_Sector  0x0032   100   100   000    Old_age   Always       -       0\n198 Offline_Uncorrectable   0x0030   100   100   000    Old_age   Offline      -       0"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix01q1", type:"mc", q:"df says the root filesystem is full (47 GB used), but du can only find about 11 GB of files. What best explains the gap?", options:["du cannot read directories owned by root, so it under-counts","The filesystem has run out of inodes, so df reports it as full","The disk is failing and reporting the wrong size to the kernel","The deleted debug.log is still open in PID 1184. Its 36 GB of blocks stay allocated until that file descriptor is closed"], answer:3, why:"lsof +L1 lists open files with a link count of 0. debug.log no longer has a name, so du, which walks the directory tree by name, cannot see it. Its inode and blocks stay allocated while python3 holds fd 3 open, and 11 GB visible plus 36 GB hidden is about the 47 GB df reports. du ran with sudo, inode use is only 5%, and SMART is clean."},
     {id:"ix01q2", type:"mc", q:"Which action frees the space fastest without rebooting the server?", options:["Run rm -rf /var/log/uploader/* to finish deleting the log","Truncate the open file through its descriptor (sudo truncate -s 0 /proc/1184/fd/3), or restart uploader.service when no upload is in progress","Run fsck on the mounted root filesystem","Turn off the ext4 reserved blocks with tune2fs -m 0 and leave the log as it is"], answer:1, why:"Truncating through /proc/PID/fd frees the blocks immediately and the process keeps running. A restart also closes the descriptor, but it cuts off any upload in progress. The file is already unlinked, so rm in that directory changes nothing. fsck on a mounted filesystem is unsafe. tune2fs -m 0 gains only about 2.5 GB, and the log keeps growing."},
     {id:"ix01q3", type:"mc", q:"Why did the log reach 36 GB in the first place?", options:["The service logs at DEBUG level and writes a line with the repr of 64 bytes for every 64 KB chunk, all to one file that never rotates","journald forwards every system message into debug.log","Python's logging module never flushes its buffer","The tablets are uploading duplicate photos"], answer:0, why:"basicConfig(filename=..., level=DEBUG) installs a plain FileHandler, which never rotates, and log.debug runs once per chunk. A 5 MB photo produces about 80 log lines, so a busy day writes gigabytes."},
     {id:"ix01q4", type:"mc", q:"How should you treat the SMART output in the coworker's note?", options:["As proof that the disk is failing and must be replaced today","As the explanation for the df/du mismatch","As a red herring: the health check PASSED with zero reallocated or pending sectors, and the mismatch has another cause","As a reason to run a long SMART self-test before touching anything else"], answer:2, why:"Nothing in the SMART data suggests a failing disk, and a dying disk would not show up as a df/du accounting gap anyway. Note the drive's age for planning and get on with fixing the space problem."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Explains the df/du gap: a deleted file that is still open (lsof +L1 shows PID 1184, fd 3, 36 GB) keeps its blocks allocated, and the numbers add up (11 GB + 36 GB ≈ 47 GB).","Frees the space safely, by truncating through /proc/1184/fd/3 or doing a planned restart of the service, and confirms it with df.","Fixes the root cause: lowers the log level to INFO and adds rotation (RotatingFileHandler, or logrotate with copytruncate or a reopen signal), and explains that deleting a log a process still has open does not free space.","Uses the evidence to rule out disk failure (SMART PASSED, 0 reallocated or pending sectors) and inode exhaustion (5% used).","Verifies the fix: test uploads succeed, df stays flat over a day, and a disk-usage alert stays in place."],
     reference:"The tablets fail with ENOSPC because / really is full. df counts allocated blocks, and that includes a file that no longer has a name. Yesterday's rm removed the directory entry for debug.log, but uploader (PID 1184) still has the file open as fd 3, so the inode and its 36 GB of blocks are still allocated. lsof +L1 shows the file with a link count of 0, and 11 GB visible plus 36 GB hidden is about the 47 GB df reports. du walks names, so it cannot see the file. Inode use is 5% and SMART is clean, so this is not hardware. Immediate fix: run sudo truncate -s 0 /proc/1184/fd/3 (or restart uploader.service when no upload is running), then check that df shows about 36 GB free and that a test upload works. Root cause: logging.basicConfig at DEBUG level, a log line for every 64 KB chunk, and a single file that never rotates. Use INFO in production, remove or rate-limit the per-chunk line, and switch to RotatingFileHandler (for example maxBytes=50 MB, backupCount=5) or logrotate with copytruncate. Keep a disk-usage alert at 80%. Lesson for the team: to empty a log that a process is writing, truncate it instead of deleting it.",
     alternatives:["Restart uploader.service during a quiet minute (closing fd 3 frees the blocks), then send logging to journald with SystemMaxUse= set so journald manages size and rotation.","Put /var/log on its own filesystem or LV, so a runaway log cannot fill / and stop uploads."] },
   hints:["df and du measure different things. What can df count that du cannot see?","A file can lose its name and still keep its blocks. Which processes have deleted files open?","Run sudo lsof +L1: PID 1184 still writes to debug.log (deleted), 36 GB. Truncate it through /proc/1184/fd/3."] },

 { id:"ix02", title:"One PON customer dark after a storm", level:2, domains:["p24","p14","p26","p2"],
   context:"You're on a GPON crew. After last night's storm, one customer fed from cabinet FDH-12 (a 1:32 splitter on OLT-3 port 1/2/4) has a red LOS light on their ONT, while the other 31 customers on that splitter are fine. Dispatch wants to know whether to send a bucket truck to the aerial drop or just swap the ONT.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"OLT-3 ONU status (excerpt)", text:"OLT-3# show gpon onu state gpon-olt_1/2/4\nOnuIndex    Admin   OMCC     Phase     Channel\n1/2/4:16    enable  enable   working   1(GPON)\n1/2/4:17    enable  disable  LOS       1(GPON)\n1/2/4:18    enable  enable   working   1(GPON)\nONU Number: 32/32\n\nOLT-3# show pon power onu-rx gpon-olt_1/2/4\nOnu         Rx power\n1/2/4:16    -21.84(dbm)\n1/2/4:17    N/A\n1/2/4:18    -22.10(dbm)\n\nOLT-3# show gpon onu detail-info gpon-olt_1/2/4:17\nVersion:          V3.1.07\nLast down cause:  LOSi\nLast down time:   2026-10-25 23:47:10\n(ONUs 16 and 18 report Version V3.4.02)"},
     {label:"Power meter readings at 1490 nm (downstream), design data and the storm log", text:"Location                              Reading\nFDH-12 splitter output, port 17       -19.6 dBm\nCustomer NID, end of drop             -31.4 dBm\n\nONT receiver sensitivity (GPON class B+): -27 dBm\nDrop as-built: FDH-12 -> 40 m underground -> riser at pole P-113 (~42 m)\n  -> aerial span to P-114 (~117 m) -> aerial to house NID (~185 m)\nDrop design loss: 2 connectors x 0.5 dB + 0.19 km x 0.35 dB/km = ~1.1 dB\n\nStorm damage log (line crew):\n23:40  Wind gusts 50+ mph, multiple trees down, Oak St.\n01:55  Limb across strand, Oak St between P-112 and P-115. Limb removed.\n02:10  Strand re-tensioned, drops re-lashed by hand. No fiber crew on site."},
     {label:"OTDR from FDH-12 port 17 toward the customer (1310/1550 nm)", text:"Pulse 10 ns   Range 2 km   IOR 1.4680   Averaging 30 s\nEvent  Dist(m)   Loss@1310(dB)  Loss@1550(dB)  Refl(dB)  Type\n1      0.0       --             --             -52.1     Launch connector\n2      42.6      0.08           0.07           --        Non-reflective\n3      118.3     5.10           13.40          --        Non-reflective\n4      184.9     --             --             -38.0     Reflective / end (NID connector)\nSpan loss:  1310 nm 5.8 dB    1550 nm 14.2 dB"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix02q1", type:"mc", q:"From the power readings, how much loss does the drop add, and what does that mean for the ONT?", options:["About 1.1 dB, within design, so the ONT itself is faulty","About 11.8 dB, roughly 10.7 dB more than designed. That leaves -31.4 dBm at the NID, below the -27 dBm sensitivity, so the ONT cannot lock","About 31.4 dB, so the OLT laser is failing","Nothing can be concluded without measuring at the OLT"], answer:1, why:"-19.6 - (-31.4) = 11.8 dB across a drop designed for about 1.1 dB. -31.4 dBm is 4.4 dB below the ONT's sensitivity, which is why it shows LOS. The splitter port reads normal and neighbors 16 and 18 are around -22 dBm, so the feeder and splitter are healthy."},
     {id:"ix02q2", type:"mc", q:"Event 3 shows 5.10 dB at 1310 nm but 13.40 dB at 1550 nm. What does that pattern indicate?", options:["A macrobend. Bend loss rises steeply with wavelength, while splice and connector losses barely change with wavelength","A bad fusion splice, which always loses more at 1550 nm","A dirty connector, since the event is non-reflective","An OTDR ghost caused by the end reflection"], answer:0, why:"Light at longer wavelengths is less tightly confined to the core, so it leaks out at a bend much more easily. A loss that is two to three times larger at 1550 nm is the classic bend signature. A dirty connector would be a reflective event, and a ghost would show no real loss."},
     {id:"ix02q3", type:"mc", q:"Where should the crew go first?", options:["The customer's NID, to re-terminate the connector","FDH-12, to replace the splitter","The aerial span near pole P-114, about 118 m of fiber from the FDH, where the storm log reports a limb on the strand","The OLT, to reseat the PON optic"], answer:2, why:"The OTDR puts the loss at 118.3 m of fiber, which the as-built places near P-114 at the end of the P-113 to P-114 span, and the line crew re-lashed drops there by hand. OTDR distance is fiber length, and slack loops can make it slightly longer than the route, so check a few metres either side."},
     {id:"ix02q4", type:"mc", q:"ONU 17 runs firmware V3.1.07, while its neighbors run V3.4.02. How should you treat that?", options:["As the root cause: push V3.4.02 now","As a reason to swap the ONT before anything else","As evidence that the OLT port is failing","Note it for a later upgrade. An ONT receiving -31.4 dBm cannot lock whatever firmware it runs, and it was working before the storm"], answer:3, why:"Firmware cannot make up for 4.4 dB of missing light, and the outage started with the storm, not with a firmware change. It is a red herring for this fault."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Isolates the fault to this customer's drop by comparing neighbors (ONUs 16 and 18 working near -22 dBm) and the normal reading at the splitter port.","Does the loss-budget arithmetic: 11.8 dB measured against about 1.1 dB designed, ending 4.4 dB below the -27 dBm sensitivity.","Reads the OTDR: a non-reflective event at about 118 m whose loss grows with wavelength is a macrobend, placed near P-114 using the as-built and the storm log.","Gives a fix and verification: relieve or re-dress the bend (replace the section if the jacket is crushed), clean and inspect the connectors, re-shoot the OTDR, check 1490 nm power at the NID (about -21 dBm), and confirm ONU 17 is 'working'.","Treats the firmware difference as a red herring and defers it."],
     reference:"The fault is in this customer's drop, not the OLT, the feeder or the splitter. Thirty-one ONUs on the same splitter are working, neighbors 16 and 18 report about -22 dBm, and splitter port 17 measures a normal -19.6 dBm. At the NID the reading is -31.4 dBm, so the drop is losing 11.8 dB against a design of about 1.1 dB (two connectors plus 0.19 km of fiber). That leaves the ONT 4.4 dB below its -27 dBm sensitivity, which is why it shows LOS. The OTDR shows where the loss is: a non-reflective event at 118.3 m that loses 5.1 dB at 1310 nm and 13.4 dB at 1550 nm. Splices and connectors lose about the same at both wavelengths, so loss that climbs steeply with wavelength is the signature of a macrobend. On the as-built, 118 m of fiber is near P-114, and the storm log says drops on the P-113 to P-114 strand were re-lashed by hand at 02:10, which probably pulled this drop into a tight bend. Send the bucket truck to P-114. Relieve the bend and re-lash the drop properly, or replace the span if the jacket is crushed. Clean and scope the connectors. To verify, re-shoot the OTDR (the event at 118 m should be gone), measure about -21 dBm at the NID, and confirm ONU 17 is 'working' at about -22 dBm on the OLT. The older firmware is a red herring. Schedule that upgrade separately.",
     alternatives:["Shine a visual fault locator into port 17 at the FDH and walk the aerial span. A bend that loses this much often glows visibly at the kink.","If the drop is badly damaged, pull a new pre-connectorized drop from P-113 to the NID and verify it end to end with a light source and power meter at 1310 and 1550 nm."] },
   hints:["Compare this customer with the 31 neighbors on the same splitter. Where can the fault be, and where can't it be?","Subtract the NID reading from the splitter-port reading and compare the result with the drop's design loss. Then look at how event 3's loss changes with wavelength.","Loss much bigger at 1550 nm than at 1310 nm means a macrobend. At 118 m of fiber that puts it near P-114, where the storm crew re-lashed drops."] },

 { id:"ix03", title:"The lab DHCP server that took over the house", level:2, domains:["p2","p3","p26"],
   context:"In your home lab, a mini-PC called labbox runs dnsmasq to hand out 10.30.0.0/24 addresses on an isolated lab VLAN (VLAN 30, switch port Gi0/6). Tonight the family's phones and the smart TV say 'Connected, no internet', but your wired desktop works fine. This afternoon you tidied the rack and re-plugged several cables.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"Phone Wi-Fi details", text:"SSID:            HomeNet\nIP address:      10.30.0.57\nSubnet mask:     255.255.255.0\nGateway:         10.30.0.1\nDNS:             10.30.0.1\nLease obtained:  today 19:02"},
     {label:"SW1 (managed switch) VLAN and port status", text:"SW1# show vlan brief\nVLAN Name      Status    Ports\n1    default   active    Gi0/1, Gi0/2, Gi0/3, Gi0/4, Gi0/5, Gi0/7, Gi0/8\n30   lab       active    Gi0/6\n\nSW1# show interfaces status\nPort   Name        Status       Vlan  Duplex  Speed\nGi0/1  isp-router  connected    1     a-full  a-1000\nGi0/2  wifi-ap     connected    1     a-full  a-1000\nGi0/3  desktop     connected    1     a-full  a-1000\nGi0/6  labbox      notconnect   30    auto    auto\nGi0/7              connected    1     a-full  a-1000"},
     {label:"labbox: journalctl -u dnsmasq, ip addr, ip route", text:"Oct 26 19:02:11 labbox dnsmasq-dhcp[812]: DHCPDISCOVER(enp1s0) 3c:28:6d:aa:41:09\nOct 26 19:02:11 labbox dnsmasq-dhcp[812]: DHCPOFFER(enp1s0) 10.30.0.57 3c:28:6d:aa:41:09\nOct 26 19:02:11 labbox dnsmasq-dhcp[812]: DHCPREQUEST(enp1s0) 10.30.0.57 3c:28:6d:aa:41:09\nOct 26 19:02:11 labbox dnsmasq-dhcp[812]: DHCPACK(enp1s0) 10.30.0.57 3c:28:6d:aa:41:09 Pixel-7\nOct 26 19:05:40 labbox dnsmasq-dhcp[812]: DHCPACK(enp1s0) 10.30.0.58 a4:77:33:10:9e:2c LivingRoom-TV\n\n$ ip -br addr show enp1s0\nenp1s0           UP             10.30.0.1/24\n$ ip route\n10.30.0.0/24 dev enp1s0 proto kernel scope link src 10.30.0.1"},
     {label:"Desktop (working) and ISP router event log", text:"desktop$ ip -4 -br addr show eno1\neno1             UP             192.168.1.142/24\ndesktop$ ip route | head -1\ndefault via 192.168.1.1 dev eno1 proto dhcp metric 100\n\nISP router event log:\n2026-10-26 03:12:44  Firmware upgraded to 4.2.18 (automatic)\n2026-10-26 03:14:02  WAN connected, IPv4 203.0.113.48\n2026-10-26 03:14:05  LAN DHCP server started, pool 192.168.1.100-192.168.1.199"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix03q1", type:"mc", q:"Why did the phone get 10.30.0.57 with gateway 10.30.0.1?", options:["The ISP router's firmware update changed its DHCP pool to 10.30.0.0/24","labbox is now plugged into Gi0/7, which is in VLAN 1 (the home LAN), so its dnsmasq answers the family's DHCP broadcasts, and it answered the phone first","The phone has a static IP left over from another network","The Wi-Fi AP is tagging home traffic into VLAN 30"], answer:1, why:"The DHCPACK to Pixel-7 is in labbox's own log. Gi0/6, the VLAN 30 port, is notconnect, while unlabeled Gi0/7 in VLAN 1 is up. The router log shows its pool is still 192.168.1.100-199."},
     {id:"ix03q2", type:"mc", q:"Why does the desktop still work?", options:["Wired connections don't use DHCP","The desktop uses IPv6 only","Its switch port has priority over the Wi-Fi AP","It still holds a valid 192.168.1.x lease, and lease renewals go unicast to the server that issued the lease (the ISP router), so labbox never had a chance to answer"], answer:3, why:"DHCPDISCOVER is a broadcast that any server on the segment can answer. A renewal is sent unicast to the server that issued the lease. The phones and the TV rejoined Wi-Fi tonight, broadcast a fresh DISCOVER, and took labbox's offer."},
     {id:"ix03q3", type:"mc", q:"What is the quickest correct fix?", options:["Move labbox's cable back to Gi0/6 (or make Gi0/7 an access port in VLAN 30), then have the affected devices forget and rejoin Wi-Fi so they get new leases","Factory-reset the ISP router","Change dnsmasq's pool to 192.168.1.100-199 so both servers agree","Turn off DHCP on the ISP router"], answer:0, why:"Putting labbox back in its own broadcast domain stops the bad offers, and forcing the clients to renew gets them proper leases. Two DHCP servers handing out the same pool would cause address conflicts, and the router is working fine."},
     {id:"ix03q4", type:"mc", q:"Which change best stops this happening again?", options:["Shorten the home DHCP lease time to one hour","Give every phone a static IP address","Enable DHCP snooping on SW1 with only the ISP-router port (Gi0/1) trusted, and label the switch ports","Turn off automatic firmware updates on the ISP router"], answer:2, why:"With DHCP snooping on, the switch drops DHCP offers and acks that arrive on untrusted ports, so a lab server in the wrong port cannot hand out leases. Labels prevent the mistake in the first place. Shorter leases would make the problem spread faster."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Recognizes 10.30.0.x addresses with gateway 10.30.0.1 as labbox's lab scope, so the leases came from dnsmasq and not the ISP router (labbox's log shows the DHCPACK to Pixel-7).","Connects the switch output: Gi0/6 (VLAN 30) is notconnect and labbox's link is up on Gi0/7 in VLAN 1, so the lab DHCP server is on the home segment.","Explains why some devices still work (existing leases renew by unicast to the ISP router), and dismisses the firmware update because the router's pool is unchanged.","Gives a fix and verification: correct the port or VLAN, make the clients renew, then check they get 192.168.1.x with gateway 192.168.1.1 and can reach the internet, and that dnsmasq logs no more ACKs to home devices.","Prevents a repeat with DHCP snooping, port labels, or a tagged lab interface."],
     reference:"The family devices aren't failing because the internet is down. They have the wrong addresses. 10.30.0.57 with gateway and DNS 10.30.0.1 is the lab's dnsmasq scope, and labbox's own log shows DHCPACKs to Pixel-7 and LivingRoom-TV tonight. The switch shows why. Gi0/6, the VLAN 30 lab port, is notconnect, while unlabeled Gi0/7 in VLAN 1 is up. During this afternoon's tidy, labbox was plugged into a home-LAN port, so its DHCP server now shares a broadcast domain with the house. A DHCPDISCOVER is a broadcast that any server on the segment may answer, and the client takes the first offer. labbox answered first. Because the lab is isolated (labbox has no default route), the clients got a gateway that leads nowhere. The desktop still works because it kept its ISP-router lease, and renewals go unicast to the server that issued the lease. The firmware update is a red herring: the router's pool is still 192.168.1.100-199. Fix it by moving labbox back to Gi0/6, or by setting Gi0/7 to access VLAN 30. Then have the phones and the TV forget and rejoin the network so they renew. Check that they get 192.168.1.x with gateway 192.168.1.1, that they can load a web page, and that dnsmasq logs no more ACKs. To prevent a repeat, label the ports and enable DHCP snooping with only the router port trusted.",
     alternatives:["Stop dnsmasq right away (sudo systemctl stop dnsmasq) to end the damage, then fix the port and start it again once labbox is back on VLAN 30.","From the desktop, run sudo nmap --script broadcast-dhcp-discover to list every DHCP server answering on the home LAN. That proves there is a second server without reading the switch config."] },
   hints:["Look at the phone's gateway address. Which device on your network uses that subnet?","Compare where labbox is supposed to be plugged in with which ports are actually up, and which VLAN each one is in.","labbox is on Gi0/7 in VLAN 1, so its dnsmasq is answering the house's DHCP broadcasts. Move it back to Gi0/6 and renew the clients."] },

 { id:"ix04", title:"A report script that got slow as data grew", level:2, domains:["p4","p14","p3","p26"],
   context:"You wrote dedupe_splices.py to flag duplicate fiber IDs in the crew's splice records before the weekly as-built report goes out. In March it finished in seconds. Now, with about 420,000 records, it runs for most of an hour on the office Linux box, and the nightly cron run is still going when the morning run starts.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"Timing runs on sample files (time python3 dedupe_splices.py sample_N.csv)", text:"records    runtime (s)\n 10,000         1.9\n 20,000         7.6\n 40,000        30.4\n 80,000       121.8\n160,000       489.5\n420,113     (full file, killed after 40 min)"},
     {label:"dedupe_splices.py (core loop)", text:"import csv, sys\n\nseen = []\ndupes = []\nwith open(sys.argv[1], newline=\"\") as f:\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\n        fid = row[\"fiber_id\"].strip().upper()\n        if fid in seen:\n            dupes.append(fid)\n        else:\n            seen.append(fid)\nprint(len(dupes), \"duplicates\")\nfor d in dupes:\n    print(d)"},
     {label:"top, iostat, a read test, and a ticket comment", text:"top - 21:14:07 up 12 days,  3 users,  load average: 1.02, 1.01, 0.97\n  PID USER   PR  NI    VIRT    RES    SHR S  %CPU  %MEM     TIME+ COMMAND\n 9311 crew   20   0   58312  41208   9840 R 100.0   0.5  31:12.44 python3\n\n$ iostat -dx 5 2 | grep -E 'Device|sda'\nDevice    r/s    rkB/s    w/s    wkB/s   %util\nsda      0.20     3.20   1.40    12.80    1.10\n\n$ nproc\n4\n$ time wc -l splices_full.csv\n420114 splices_full.csv\nreal    0m0.142s\n\nTicket comment from a coworker: 'That box still has a 5400 rpm hard drive. Let's order an SSD, that'll fix it.'"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix04q1", type:"mc", q:"What growth pattern do the timing runs show?", options:["Linear: the time doubles when the records double","n log n: a little more than double each time","Quadratic: each doubling multiplies the runtime by about 4","Exponential: the time squares each time"], answer:2, why:"7.6/1.9 = 4.0, 30.4/7.6 = 4.0, 121.8/30.4 = 4.0 and 489.5/121.8 = 4.02. A steady ratio of 4 per doubling is the signature of O(n^2), because (2n)^2 = 4n^2."},
     {id:"ix04q2", type:"mc", q:"Which line causes that growth?", options:["if fid in seen: a membership test on a Python list compares against every element, so n records cost about n^2/2 comparisons","csv.DictReader, which re-reads the file for every row","row[\"fiber_id\"].strip().upper(), which creates new strings","print(d), because printing is slow"], answer:0, why:"seen grows by one entry for each unique ID, and 'in' on a list is a linear scan. Record k costs about k comparisons, so the total is 1+2+...+n ≈ n^2/2. The other lines do a constant amount of work per row."},
     {id:"ix04q3", type:"mc", q:"At the current growth, about how long would the full 420,000-record file take?", options:["About 20 minutes","About 8 hours","About 2 minutes","About 56 minutes"], answer:3, why:"Scale the 160k run by the square of the size ratio: 489.5 s × (420/160)^2 = 489.5 × 6.89 ≈ 3,370 s, about 56 minutes. That matches 'most of an hour'."},
     {id:"ix04q4", type:"mc", q:"Will the SSD fix the problem?", options:["Yes: the hard drive is the bottleneck","No: one core sits at 100% CPU while the disk is about 1% utilized, and wc reads the whole file in 0.14 s, so the job is CPU-bound in the algorithm","Yes, if the box also gets more RAM","Only if the CSV is split into smaller files"], answer:1, why:"An I/O-bound job would show high %util and low CPU. Here it's the other way round, and the job uses only 41 MB of RAM. Splitting the file wouldn't help either, because duplicates have to be checked across the whole set."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Reads the timing table: a ratio of about 4 per doubling means O(n^2) growth, and projects about 56 minutes for 420k records.","Names the cause: list membership is O(n) and runs once per row, giving O(n^2) in total.","Uses top, iostat and wc to show the job is CPU-bound, not disk-bound, and rejects the SSD idea.","Fixes it with a set (or dict or Counter) for O(1) average lookups, keeps the output order if the report depends on it, and checks the new output is identical to the old on a sample file.","Re-times the script to show linear growth, and stops cron runs from overlapping (for example with flock)."],
     reference:"The timing table answers the main question. Every time the input doubles, the runtime goes up almost exactly 4 times (1.9, 7.6, 30.4, 121.8, 489.5 s). That is quadratic growth. Projecting from 160k to 420k records gives 489.5 × (420/160)^2 ≈ 3,370 s, about 56 minutes, which matches the complaint. The cause is the line 'if fid in seen', where seen is a list. A list membership test scans element by element, so record k costs about k comparisons, and the whole file costs about n^2/2: roughly 88 billion comparisons for 420k rows. The machine data agrees. python3 holds one core at 100%, the disk sits at about 1% utilization, and wc reads the whole file in 0.14 s. A faster disk would change nothing, so don't order the SSD. Fix it by making seen a set (seen = set(); seen.add(fid)). A hash lookup is O(1) on average, so the whole job becomes O(n), and 420k rows should take about a second. Keep dupes as a list so the report order doesn't change. To verify, run the old and new versions on sample_40000.csv and diff the outputs, then rerun the timing table and confirm the times now roughly double instead of quadrupling. Finally, wrap the cron command in flock -n so two runs can never overlap.",
     alternatives:["Use collections.Counter over all fiber IDs and report the IDs with a count above 1. That is also O(n), and it gives duplicate counts for the report.","Load the CSV with pandas and select rows where df.fiber_id.str.strip().str.upper().duplicated() is True. That is vectorized and runs in linear time."] },
   hints:["Divide each runtime by the one before it. What does a steady ratio of about 4 tell you?","Which operation inside the loop gets slower as the data grows?","'fid in seen' scans the whole list every time. Make seen a set and the job becomes linear."] },

 { id:"ix05", title:"API workers killed every night", level:3, domains:["p3","p15","p4","p5"],
   context:"You run the crew's job-ticket API: Flask under gunicorn (4 sync workers) behind nginx on api01, a 4 GB Ubuntu VM with no swap. The service is redeployed, and so restarted, every morning at 07:30. For a week, sometime between 02:30 and 03:00, the API has returned 502 and 504 errors for about a minute and then recovered on its own. Someone suspects the nightly logrotate.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"journalctl -k (kernel messages) around the outage", text:"Oct 26 02:41:07 api01 kernel: gunicorn invoked oom-killer: gfp_mask=0x140cca(GFP_HIGHUSER_MOVABLE|__GFP_COMP), order=0, oom_score_adj=0\nOct 26 02:41:07 api01 kernel: Free swap  = 0kB\nOct 26 02:41:07 api01 kernel: Total swap = 0kB\nOct 26 02:41:07 api01 kernel: [  pid  ]   uid  tgid total_vm      rss pgtables_bytes swapents oom_score_adj name\nOct 26 02:41:07 api01 kernel: [   1022]  1001  1022     8901     5210    94208        0             0 gunicorn\nOct 26 02:41:07 api01 kernel: [   1028]  1001  1028   251220   229810  1875968        0             0 gunicorn\nOct 26 02:41:07 api01 kernel: [   1029]  1001  1029   248760   226455  1851392        0             0 gunicorn\nOct 26 02:41:07 api01 kernel: [   1030]  1001  1030   255118   233902  1908736        0             0 gunicorn\nOct 26 02:41:07 api01 kernel: [   1031]  1001  1031   262441   240117  2035712        0             0 gunicorn\nOct 26 02:41:07 api01 kernel: oom-kill:constraint=CONSTRAINT_NONE,nodemask=(null),cpuset=/,mems_allowed=0,global_oom,task_memcg=/system.slice/gunicorn.service,task=gunicorn,pid=1031,uid=1001\nOct 26 02:41:07 api01 kernel: Out of memory: Killed process 1031 (gunicorn) total-vm:1049764kB, anon-rss:960468kB, file-rss:0kB, shmem-rss:0kB, UID:1001 pgtables:1988kB oom_score_adj:0"},
     {label:"Per-worker RSS since the morning restart (ps -o pid,rss sampled by cron, MB)", text:"time    pid1028  pid1029  pid1030  pid1031\n08:00       182      176      190      185\n12:00       348      331      362      355\n16:00       521      498      540      530\n20:00       690      661      712      703\n00:00       842      811      866      858\n02:30       918      905      934      951"},
     {label:"nginx error.log, and journalctl around 02:00 (the logrotate theory)", text:"2026/10/26 02:40:12 [error] 880#880: *55102 upstream timed out (110: Connection timed out) while reading response header from upstream, client: 192.0.2.44, server: api.example.com, request: \"GET /tickets?since=2026-10-26T02:38:51Z&crew=7 HTTP/1.1\", upstream: \"http://127.0.0.1:8000/tickets?since=2026-10-26T02:38:51Z&crew=7\"\n2026/10/26 02:41:07 [error] 880#880: *55120 upstream prematurely closed connection while reading response header from upstream, client: 198.51.100.23, server: api.example.com, request: \"GET /tickets?since=2026-10-26T02:39:40Z&crew=3 HTTP/1.1\", upstream: \"http://127.0.0.1:8000/tickets?since=2026-10-26T02:39:40Z&crew=3\"\n\n$ journalctl --since 01:59 --until 02:01\nOct 26 02:00:01 api01 systemd[1]: Starting logrotate.service - Rotate log files...\nOct 26 02:00:01 api01 systemd[1]: Reloading nginx.service - A high performance web server and a reverse proxy server...\nOct 26 02:00:01 api01 systemd[1]: Reloaded nginx.service - A high performance web server and a reverse proxy server.\nOct 26 02:00:02 api01 systemd[1]: logrotate.service: Deactivated successfully."},
     {label:"app/views.py (excerpt)", text:"_ticket_cache = {}   # module level: speeds up repeat queries\n\n@app.get(\"/tickets\")\ndef tickets():\n    key = (request.args.get(\"since\", \"\"),\n           request.args.get(\"crew\", \"\"),\n           request.args.get(\"page\", \"1\"))\n    if key not in _ticket_cache:\n        _ticket_cache[key] = query_tickets(*key)   # list of dicts, often 1-5 MB\n    return jsonify(_ticket_cache[key])"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix05q1", type:"mc", q:"What actually ends the outage at 02:41:07?", options:["logrotate restarts gunicorn","The kernel's OOM killer kills gunicorn worker 1031 (anon-rss about 940 MB) because RAM is exhausted and there is no swap. The gunicorn master then starts a fresh worker","nginx reloads and drops its connections","systemd hits the unit's MemoryMax and restarts the service"], answer:1, why:"The kernel log says 'Out of memory: Killed process 1031', and constraint=global_oom means the whole machine ran out, not a cgroup limit like MemoryMax. logrotate finished at 02:00:02 and only reloaded nginx gracefully. The upstream timeouts just before the kill are the VM thrashing as memory runs out."},
     {id:"ix05q2", type:"mc", q:"Why do all four workers grow at nearly the same rate?", options:["They share one cache through shared memory","The Python GIL makes them allocate in lockstep","nginx sends every request to all four workers","Each forked worker is a separate process with its own private copy of _ticket_cache. Requests are spread evenly, so every copy grows independently"], answer:3, why:"gunicorn forks its workers. After the fork, module-level objects like _ticket_cache are per-process, and copy-on-write pages become private as soon as they are written. With even load balancing, four private caches fill at about the same rate, using four times the memory."},
     {id:"ix05q3", type:"mc", q:"Why does this cache never stop growing?", options:["Its key includes the since timestamp, which is different on almost every sync call, so nearly every request adds a new 1-5 MB entry and nothing is ever evicted","Flask never frees response objects","jsonify leaks memory","Python cannot free dict entries"], answer:0, why:"A cache only saves memory and time when keys repeat. Here the key is almost always new (since=2026-10-26T02:38:51Z, then 02:39:40Z...) and the dict has no size limit or expiry, so it simply accumulates every result ever returned."},
     {id:"ix05q4", type:"mc", q:"Which change fixes the root cause rather than the symptom?", options:["Add a 4 GB swap file","Set oom_score_adj=-1000 on gunicorn so the kernel kills something else","Replace the dict with a bounded cache (LRU with a maxsize and a TTL) on stable keys, or drop it and cache with an expiry in Redis, using gunicorn --max-requests with jitter as a stopgap","Double the number of workers so each one holds less"], answer:2, why:"Swap only delays the crash and adds thrashing. oom_score_adj=-1000 makes the kernel kill sshd or nginx instead. More workers means more private caches. Bounding or removing the cache stops the growth. max-requests recycles workers before they bloat while the code fix ships."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Finds the OOM kill in the kernel log and lines its time up with the 502s. Explains global_oom with no swap, and the thrashing before it (the nginx timeouts).","Rules out logrotate with evidence: it finished at 02:00:02 and only reloaded nginx gracefully.","Reads the per-worker RSS table as steady growth in every forked worker since the 07:30 restart, which points to a leak in the app, not a single bad request.","Finds the unbounded module-level dict keyed on the unique since timestamp, and explains why the memory never comes back.","Gives a fix and verification: a bounded LRU or TTL cache (or an external cache with expiry) plus --max-requests with jitter as a stopgap, then RSS staying flat over 24 hours (tracemalloc or ps sampling) and no oom-kill entries in journalctl -k."],
     reference:"The kernel is killing the API, not logrotate. At 02:41:07 the kernel log shows a global OOM with no swap. It lists four gunicorn workers of roughly 900 MB each and kills PID 1031. The 504s just before that are the VM thrashing: it evicts page cache as it runs out of RAM, so requests time out until the kill frees memory. logrotate finished at 02:00:02 and only reloaded nginx gracefully, so it's a red herring. The RSS table shows the mechanism. Since the 07:30 restart, every worker has grown at the same steady 40-50 MB an hour, until four of them no longer fit in 4 GB. Steady growth in every process means a leak in the code, and views.py has it: a module-level dict that caches whole result lists keyed on (since, crew, page). Offline-sync clients send a new since timestamp on every call, so almost every request adds a new 1-5 MB entry that is never evicted. Each forked worker keeps its own copy, which is why all four grow together. The fix is to delete the dict or replace it with a bounded cache such as cachetools.TTLCache(maxsize=256, ttl=60), and to stop caching per-timestamp queries at all. As a stopgap, run gunicorn with --max-requests 2000 --max-requests-jitter 200 to recycle workers. To verify, graph per-worker RSS for 24 hours (it should level off) and confirm journalctl -k shows no new oom-kill lines.",
     alternatives:["On a staging copy, take tracemalloc (or memray) snapshots while replaying a night of /tickets traffic, and compare the top allocations to prove the dict is the leak before changing code.","Set MemoryHigh and MemoryMax on the gunicorn unit so a leak hits a cgroup limit and restarts only the API instead of thrashing the whole VM. That buys time while the real fix ships."] },
   hints:["502s that clear up by themselves at about the same time every night: what does the kernel log say for that minute?","Look at how each worker's memory changes through the day. A steady climb in every process points to the code, not to traffic spikes.","_ticket_cache is a dict that never evicts, keyed on a since timestamp that is new on almost every call. Bound it or remove it."] },
 { id:"ix06", title:"The five-second first request", level:3, domains:["p2","p3","p5","p26"],
   context:"After the office network moved to a new router over the weekend, the internal inventory web app on app02 has become 'randomly slow'. Some page loads take just over 5 seconds and others are instant, and the developer says nothing in the code changed. Every page calls an internal pricing service at pricing.corp.example.com. You're asked whether the problem is in the app.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"app02 application log (excerpt)", text:"2026-10-26 09:14:02,118 WARNING inventory.views pricing lookup took 5.031s part=SPL-0042\n2026-10-26 09:14:02,119 WARNING py.warnings DeprecationWarning: datetime.datetime.utcnow() is deprecated and scheduled for removal in a future version. Use timezone-aware objects to represent datetimes in UTC: datetime.datetime.now(datetime.UTC).\n2026-10-26 09:14:07,402 INFO    inventory.views pricing lookup took 0.019s part=SPL-0043\n2026-10-26 09:14:11,950 INFO    inventory.views pricing lookup took 0.021s part=SPL-0044\n2026-10-26 09:14:19,377 WARNING inventory.views pricing lookup took 5.027s part=SPL-0045\n2026-10-26 09:14:25,016 INFO    inventory.views pricing lookup took 0.018s part=SPL-0046"},
     {label:"curl timing from app02", text:"$ for i in $(seq 6); do curl -s -o /dev/null -w 'dns=%{time_namelookup} connect=%{time_connect} total=%{time_total}\\n' http://pricing.corp.example.com:8080/health; done\ndns=5.004121 connect=5.005077 total=5.019846\ndns=0.001877 connect=0.002690 total=0.015230\ndns=0.001902 connect=0.002811 total=0.014977\ndns=5.003655 connect=5.004498 total=5.018204\ndns=5.004010 connect=5.004917 total=5.019551\ndns=0.001795 connect=0.002604 total=0.015112\n\n$ curl -s -o /dev/null -w 'total=%{time_total}\\n' http://192.168.20.40:8080/health\ntotal=0.013402"},
     {label:"/etc/resolv.conf on app02, each resolver tested directly, and the cutover notes", text:"$ cat /etc/resolv.conf\n# static - set by hand 2024-03 (ticket OPS-118)\nsearch corp.example.com\nnameserver 192.168.10.1\nnameserver 192.168.20.1\noptions rotate timeout:5 attempts:2\n\n$ dig @192.168.10.1 pricing.corp.example.com +time=2 +tries=1\n;; communications error to 192.168.10.1#53: timed out\n;; no servers could be reached\n\n$ dig @192.168.20.1 pricing.corp.example.com +short\n192.168.20.40\n\nWeekend cutover notes:\n- Old router rtr-old (192.168.10.1) powered off Saturday 18:00\n- New router rtr-new (192.168.20.1): gateway, DHCP, DNS forwarder\n- Servers re-addressed to 192.168.20.0/24 by hand; default gateway updated on each\n- DHCP clients picked up the new DNS server automatically"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix06q1", type:"mc", q:"What does a delay of almost exactly 5.00 s point to?", options:["A TCP retransmission timeout on the connection to the pricing service","The pricing service's database being slow on some queries","Python's GIL blocking the request thread","A resolver timeout. The time is spent in name lookup (dns=5.004), and it matches timeout:5 in resolv.conf"], answer:3, why:"curl splits the time. In the slow runs, name lookup alone takes 5.004 s and connecting adds 1 ms. Calling the IP directly is always fast, and a delay that lands exactly on a configured timeout is a strong clue."},
     {id:"ix06q2", type:"mc", q:"Why are only some requests slow?", options:["options rotate makes glibc spread queries across the listed nameservers. A lookup that starts with the dead 192.168.10.1 waits the full 5 s timeout before trying 192.168.20.1","The pricing service caches some answers","Random packet loss on the new switch","ARP entries expiring on app02"], answer:0, why:"Without rotate, every lookup would hit the dead server first and every request would be slow. With rotate, lookups that happen to go to 192.168.20.1 first come back in 2 ms, so you see a mix of fast and slow."},
     {id:"ix06q3", type:"mc", q:"What is the right fix?", options:["Raise the timeout to 10 seconds so lookups fail less often","Put pricing.corp.example.com in /etc/hosts on app02","Replace the dead 192.168.10.1 with the new router (or another working resolver) in app02's persistent network config, drop rotate unless every listed server is healthy, and check the other hand-configured servers for the same stale entry","Add retry logic to the app's HTTP client"], answer:2, why:"The fault is a stale resolver entry left over from the migration. /etc/hosts fixes one name on one machine and creates a new trap. Longer timeouts and retries just hide or worsen the delay. Other servers re-addressed by hand probably have the same entry."},
     {id:"ix06q4", type:"mc", q:"The log also shows a DeprecationWarning for datetime.utcnow(). What does it tell you about this incident?", options:["It's the cause: deprecated calls run slowly","Nothing. It's a harmless warning to fix in normal maintenance: Python prints it once per call site, it doesn't change timing, and curl reproduces the 5 s delay with no Python involved","The Python upgrade broke DNS","The app has to be restarted to clear it"], answer:1, why:"It's a red herring. It appears next to a slow request because both were logged at that moment, and the curl tests prove the delay happens outside the app."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Splits the request time (curl -w or app timing) to show the delay is in name lookup and matches the 5 s resolver timeout, and that calling the IP directly is always fast.","Tests each resolver directly with dig: 192.168.10.1 times out and 192.168.20.1 answers.","Explains the intermittency with options rotate, and ties the stale entry to the router migration (a static resolv.conf from 2024).","Fixes it persistently through whatever manages resolv.conf, then re-runs the timing loop (every lookup under 50 ms) and audits the other statically configured hosts.","Shows the DeprecationWarning and the app code are unrelated (curl reproduces the delay with no Python involved)."],
     reference:"This is a DNS problem that looks like an app bug. curl's timing breakdown shows the slow runs spend 5.004 s in time_namelookup and about a millisecond connecting, and a request straight to the IP 192.168.20.40 is always fast. A delay of almost exactly 5.00 s is a configured timeout, and resolv.conf on app02 has timeout:5. That file was set by hand in 2024 and still lists the old router, 192.168.10.1, which was switched off on Saturday. dig confirms it: 192.168.10.1 times out and 192.168.20.1 answers. The 'random' part comes from options rotate. glibc spreads lookups across the listed nameservers, so some go to the dead server first, wait 5 s and then fall back, while others go straight to 192.168.20.1 and finish in 2 ms. DHCP clients picked up the new DNS automatically, which is why only hand-configured servers like app02 are affected. The DeprecationWarning is noise, since curl reproduces the delay with no Python involved. Make the fix in whatever owns the file (netplan, NetworkManager or config management) so it survives a reboot: point app02 at 192.168.20.1, plus a second healthy resolver if you have one, and drop rotate. Then re-run the six-request loop and confirm every dns= value is a few milliseconds and the 'pricing lookup took' warnings stop. Finally, check the other re-addressed servers for 192.168.10.1.",
     alternatives:["Install a local caching resolver (systemd-resolved or unbound) on app02 that forwards to 192.168.20.1. It hides upstream latency and fails over between upstreams faster than glibc does.","As a short-term workaround, set options timeout:1 attempts:1 while the proper change is scheduled. That cuts the stall to about a second and confirms the theory, but the stale server still needs removing."] },
   hints:["The slow requests take almost exactly 5.00 s. What on the system has a 5-second timeout?","Use curl -w to split each request into lookup, connect and transfer time, then query each nameserver on its own with dig.","resolv.conf still lists the old router 192.168.10.1, which is switched off, and options rotate sends some lookups there first. Replace it."] },

 { id:"ix07", title:"Field gateway that lost track of time", level:3, domains:["p10","p25","p2","p3"],
   context:"A Raspberry Pi 4 (cab-0417) in a roadside equipment cabinet reads the cabinet's temperature and door sensors and uploads the readings over HTTPS to ingest.example.com through the cabinet's LTE router. It runs a read-only overlay root filesystem to protect its SD card. After a power cut last night the Pi booted again, but every upload now fails. The server team renewed the ingest certificate two days ago and says other sites are uploading fine.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"journalctl -u uploader -n 6 (on the Pi, read on Monday 26 Oct)", text:"Oct 16 23:58:31 cab-0417 uploader[611]: starting; 412 readings queued on disk since last successful upload\nOct 16 23:58:32 cab-0417 uploader[611]: resolved ingest.example.com -> 203.0.113.80\nOct 16 23:59:02 cab-0417 uploader[611]: POST https://ingest.example.com/v1/readings failed\nOct 16 23:59:02 cab-0417 uploader[611]: requests.exceptions.SSLError: HTTPSConnectionPool(host='ingest.example.com', port=443): Max retries exceeded with url: /v1/readings (Caused by SSLError(SSLCertVerificationError(1, '[SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED] certificate verify failed: certificate is not yet valid (_ssl.c:1006)')))\nOct 16 23:59:32 cab-0417 uploader[611]: retrying in 30 s (attempt 2)\nOct 16 23:59:32 cab-0417 uploader[611]: LTE signal RSRP -112 dBm (weak)"},
     {label:"timedatectl and fake-hwclock (on the Pi)", text:"$ timedatectl\n               Local time: Fri 2026-10-16 23:59:40 CDT\n           Universal time: Sat 2026-10-17 04:59:40 UTC\n                 RTC time: n/a\n                Time zone: America/Chicago (CDT, -0500)\nSystem clock synchronized: no\n              NTP service: active\n          RTC in local mode: no\n\n$ cat /etc/fake-hwclock.data\n2026-10-17 04:58:01\n$ findmnt -no FSTYPE /\noverlay"},
     {label:"systemd-timesyncd log and the server certificate (from the Pi)", text:"Oct 16 23:58:12 cab-0417 systemd-timesyncd[402]: Timed out waiting for reply from 192.0.2.123:123 (0.pool.example.net).\nOct 16 23:58:22 cab-0417 systemd-timesyncd[402]: Timed out waiting for reply from 198.51.100.7:123 (1.pool.example.net).\n\n$ openssl s_client -connect ingest.example.com:443 -servername ingest.example.com </dev/null 2>&1 | grep -E 'depth=0|verify error'\ndepth=0 CN = ingest.example.com\nverify error:num=9:certificate is not yet valid\n\n$ openssl s_client -connect ingest.example.com:443 -servername ingest.example.com </dev/null 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -dates\nnotBefore=Oct 24 14:03:11 2026 GMT\nnotAfter=Jan 22 14:03:10 2027 GMT"},
     {label:"Cabinet LTE router outbound firewall", text:"cab-0417-lte# show firewall outbound\nSeq  Action  Proto  Destination     Dport  Comment\n10   permit  tcp    any             443    https uploads (CHG-2291, 2026-10-14)\n20   permit  udp    203.0.113.53    53     dns\n30   deny    any    any             any    default deny (CHG-2291)\n\ncab-0417-lte# show firewall counters seq 30\nSeq 30 deny: 2,814 packets   top destination port: udp/123"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix07q1", type:"mc", q:"What does 'certificate is not yet valid' mean here?", options:["The certificate has expired","The Pi's clock says 17 October, but the renewed certificate's notBefore is 24 October, so from the Pi's point of view the certificate isn't valid yet","The Pi is missing the CA's root certificate","The server sent a certificate for the wrong hostname"], answer:1, why:"Validity dates are checked against the local clock. Other sites, with correct clocks, accept the same certificate. A missing root would fail with 'unable to get local issuer certificate', and a wrong name would be a hostname mismatch."},
     {id:"ix07q2", type:"mc", q:"Why did the Pi's clock come back about 10 days behind after the power cut?", options:["The time zone was reset to UTC","The RTC battery is flat","A Pi 4 has no hardware RTC. fake-hwclock restores the last saved time at boot, and on the read-only overlay root that file hasn't been updated on the SD card since 17 October","The LTE router served the wrong time"], answer:2, why:"timedatectl shows 'RTC time: n/a'. fake-hwclock does save the time regularly, but on an overlay root those writes go to RAM and are lost at power-off, so every cold boot starts from the same stale timestamp."},
     {id:"ix07q3", type:"mc", q:"Why hasn't the clock corrected itself since boot?", options:["NTP (UDP 123) is blocked. CHG-2291 permits only TCP 443 and DNS outbound and then denies everything else, so timesyncd's requests time out","timesyncd is disabled","The weak LTE signal is dropping NTP packets","NTP refuses to correct an offset of more than 1000 seconds"], answer:0, why:"timesyncd logs 'Timed out waiting for reply ... :123', and the router's default-deny counter lists udp/123 as its top port. The LTE link is weak but working: DNS resolves and the TLS handshake gets far enough to receive the certificate. timesyncd steps the clock on its first successful sync."},
     {id:"ix07q4", type:"mc", q:"Which fix is right?", options:["Set verify=False in the uploader until the clock is fixed","Ask the server team to reissue the certificate with an earlier notBefore","Set the clock by hand with date -s and move on","Allow outbound UDP 123 to the NTP servers (or serve NTP from the LTE router), start the uploader only after time sync (systemd-time-wait-sync / time-sync.target), and add an RTC or keep fake-hwclock outside the overlay. Then check the other cabinets on CHG-2291"], answer:3, why:"Turning off verification removes the protection TLS exists to give. Backdating the certificate or setting the clock by hand leaves the next power cut to fail the same way. The real fix restores a time source, makes boot order respect it, and checks every site that got the same change."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Reads the TLS error precisely ('not yet valid' is not 'expired') and compares the certificate's notBefore (24 Oct) with the Pi's clock (17 Oct).","Explains why the clock is wrong: no RTC, fake-hwclock restoring a stale timestamp, and an overlay root that loses updates at power-off.","Finds that CHG-2291's default deny blocks NTP (timesyncd timeouts, deny counter topped by udp/123), which is why the clock never corrected itself.","Fixes it without weakening TLS: allows NTP or provides a local time source, orders the uploader after time sync, and considers an RTC. Never uses verify=False.","Verifies the result (System clock synchronized: yes, uploads return 2xx, the backlog drains), checks the other cabinets on the same change, and dismisses the weak LTE signal with evidence."],
     reference:"The certificate is fine. The Pi's clock is wrong. The uploader fails with 'certificate is not yet valid', openssl shows the renewed certificate's notBefore is 24 October, and timedatectl on the Pi says it is 17 October. Validity is checked against the local clock, so to this Pi the new certificate doesn't start for another week, while sites with correct clocks accept it. Even the journal's own timestamps say Oct 16. Two faults combine here. First, a Pi 4 has no RTC ('RTC time: n/a'), so after the power cut fake-hwclock restored its saved timestamp, and because the root is an overlay, the copy on the SD card hasn't changed since 17 October. Second, the Pi can't correct itself. CHG-2291 on the LTE router permits only TCP 443 and DNS, then denies everything else, and the deny counter's top port is udp/123, matching timesyncd's timeouts. Before the renewal, the old certificate's notBefore was months back, so the bad clock went unnoticed. The LTE signal is weak but working: DNS and the TLS handshake succeed. Fix: permit UDP 123 to the NTP servers, or let the LTE router serve NTP. Make uploader.service wait for time-sync.target with systemd-time-wait-sync enabled. Plan an RTC module, or keep fake-hwclock.data on persistent storage. Never set verify=False. Verify with 'System clock synchronized: yes' and a 2xx upload, then check every cabinet covered by CHG-2291.",
     alternatives:["Use the LTE router as the time source. Many cellular routers can take time from the carrier network or GPS and serve NTP on the LAN, which keeps the outbound rules tight.","Add a battery-backed RTC HAT (for example one based on the DS3231), so the clock survives power cuts even before the network comes up."] },
   hints:["Read the TLS error word for word. 'Not yet valid' is a different complaint from 'expired'.","Compare the date on the Pi with the certificate's notBefore, then ask why the Pi's clock can't fix itself.","No RTC plus a stale fake-hwclock on a read-only root put the clock back to 17 October, and CHG-2291 blocks UDP 123. Allow NTP and make the uploader wait for time sync."] },

 { id:"ix08", title:"Order history page timing out", level:3, domains:["p18","p13","p5"],
   context:"The parts-ordering web app's 'My orders' page has gone from instant to 8-12 seconds for some customers, and the reverse proxy's 10-second timeout has started returning 504s. The orders table in PostgreSQL 16 now holds about 4.2 million rows. A ticket comment suggests raising work_mem because 'the sort is slow'.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"App request timing (structured log) and the PostgreSQL slow query log", text:"path=/orders customer=48213 status=200 total_ms=9431 db_ms=9412 render_ms=14 serialize_ms=3\npath=/orders customer=10077 status=200 total_ms=9180 db_ms=9161 render_ms=12 serialize_ms=4\npath=/orders customer=31540 status=200 total_ms=8822 db_ms=8805 render_ms=13 serialize_ms=3\npath=/orders customer=48213 status=504 total_ms=10000 note=proxy_timeout\n\n2026-10-26 10:02:17.441 UTC [23811] LOG:  duration: 9412.338 ms  statement: SELECT id, part_no, qty, status, created_at FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 48213 ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 25;"},
     {label:"EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) for that statement", text:"Limit  (cost=389214.55..389217.47 rows=25 width=52) (actual time=9398.112..9410.305 rows=25 loops=1)\n  Buffers: shared hit=1204 read=231876\n  ->  Gather Merge  (cost=389214.55..389301.40 rows=744 width=52) (actual time=9398.110..9410.296 rows=25 loops=1)\n        Workers Planned: 2\n        Workers Launched: 2\n        ->  Sort  (cost=388214.53..388215.46 rows=372 width=52) (actual time=9385.441..9385.452 rows=21 loops=3)\n              Sort Key: created_at DESC\n              Sort Method: top-N heapsort  Memory: 27kB\n              ->  Parallel Seq Scan on orders  (cost=0.00..388203.60 rows=372 width=52) (actual time=12.904..9384.877 rows=290 loops=3)\n                    Filter: (customer_id = 48213)\n                    Rows Removed by Filter: 1399710\nPlanning Time: 0.311 ms\nExecution Time: 9410.402 ms"},
     {label:"\\d orders", text:"                       Table \"public.orders\"\n   Column    |           Type           | Nullable |              Default\n-------------+--------------------------+----------+------------------------------------\n id          | bigint                   | not null | nextval('orders_id_seq'::regclass)\n customer_id | integer                  | not null |\n part_no     | text                     | not null |\n qty         | integer                  | not null |\n status      | text                     | not null |\n created_at  | timestamp with time zone | not null | now()\nIndexes:\n    \"orders_pkey\" PRIMARY KEY, btree (id)\n    \"orders_status_idx\" btree (status)"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix08q1", type:"mc", q:"Where is the 9.4 seconds going?", options:["Sorting the results, which is why work_mem matters","Query planning","Sending 25 rows over the network","A parallel sequential scan that reads the whole 4.2M-row table (about 232,000 buffers from disk) to find roughly 870 rows for this customer"], answer:3, why:"The Seq Scan node finishes at 9384.9 ms, and the Sort above it adds about 0.6 ms. rows=290 × 3 loops ≈ 870 matches, and Rows Removed by Filter 1,399,710 × 3 ≈ 4.2M rows rejected. read=231876 8 kB pages is about 1.8 GB. Planning took 0.3 ms."},
     {id:"ix08q2", type:"mc", q:"Which index best serves this query?", options:["CREATE INDEX ON orders (status, created_at)","CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ON orders (customer_id, created_at DESC)","CREATE INDEX ON orders (created_at)","A hash index on part_no"], answer:1, why:"The equality column goes first, then the ORDER BY column. Postgres descends the B-tree to customer 48213's newest entry, reads 25 entries in order and stops, with no sort and no scan. CONCURRENTLY builds it without blocking writes. An index on created_at alone would walk every customer's recent orders, and status and part_no aren't in the WHERE clause."},
     {id:"ix08q3", type:"mc", q:"Why won't raising work_mem help?", options:["The sort is a top-N heapsort using 27 kB. It never spills to disk and takes well under a millisecond, so the time is all in the scan","work_mem only applies to INSERT statements","Postgres ignores work_mem in parallel queries","It would help, but it needs a server restart"], answer:0, why:"work_mem limits memory for sorts and hashes before they spill to disk. This sort keeps only the top 25 rows in a small heap, so it has nothing to gain."},
     {id:"ix08q4", type:"mc", q:"With the composite index in place, how does this query's cost change as the table grows from 4.2M to 42M rows?", options:["It grows about 10× because the table is 10× bigger","It grows quadratically","It stays roughly constant: an O(log n) descent (the tree may gain a level) plus reading 25 entries","It becomes slower than a sequential scan"], answer:2, why:"A B-tree with a fan-out of hundreds needs only 3-4 levels for tens of millions of rows. The work is the descent plus k = 25 leaf entries, so O(log n + k), compared with O(n) for the scan."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Reads EXPLAIN ANALYZE correctly: the parallel seq scan accounts for almost all the time (about 4.2M rows filtered, about 232k buffers read), and the sort is trivial.","Chooses a composite B-tree index (customer_id, created_at DESC) and explains the column order in terms of WHERE equality, ORDER BY and LIMIT. Notes the existing status index is useless here.","Builds it safely: CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY off-peak, checks disk space, runs ANALYZE, and confirms the index is valid afterwards.","Verifies with a fresh EXPLAIN ANALYZE (Index Scan under the Limit, milliseconds) and the app's p95 or 504 rate, and rejects work_mem with evidence.","Mentions the trade-off: an extra index costs some write throughput and disk space."],
     reference:"The app log puts almost all the time in the database (db_ms 9412 out of 9431). EXPLAIN shows why. No index covers customer_id, so Postgres runs a parallel sequential scan over the whole 4.2-million-row table. It reads about 232,000 pages (about 1.8 GB) from disk and throws away 4.2 million rows to keep about 870. The scan node finishes at 9,385 ms, and the Sort above it adds under a millisecond: a top-N heapsort in 27 kB that never touches disk. That rules out work_mem. The existing orders_status_idx doesn't help because status isn't in the WHERE clause. The cost grows with the table, which is why the page slowed down as orders built up. The fix is a composite index that matches the query: CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY orders_customer_created_idx ON orders (customer_id, created_at DESC). The equality column comes first and the sort column second, so Postgres descends the B-tree straight to customer 48213's newest order, reads 25 entries already in order, and stops. That is O(log n + 25) instead of O(n), and it stays fast as the table grows. Build it off-peak, check free disk space first, run ANALYZE orders, and re-run EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS). Expect an Index Scan under the Limit, a few dozen buffers and about a millisecond of execution time. Then watch the page's p95 and the 504 count. The index adds a little cost to every insert, which is fine for a read-heavy page.",
     alternatives:["Add INCLUDE (part_no, qty, status) to the index to make it covering. Postgres can then use an index-only scan for these 25 rows, at the cost of a larger index.","If customers mostly view recent orders, partition orders by month on created_at so old partitions are pruned, and still add the composite index in each partition."] },
   hints:["In EXPLAIN ANALYZE, find the node whose actual time accounts for almost all of the 9.4 s.","Compare the rows the scan keeps with the rows it removes. What does the query filter on, and what does it sort by?","A Seq Scan that removes 4.2M rows: create an index on (customer_id, created_at DESC) so the LIMIT 25 can stop early."] },

 { id:"ix09", title:"CRC errors and OSPF flaps on a fiber ring", level:3, domains:["p24","p27","p2","p26"],
   context:"Your company runs a small metro ring of three huts, with routers R1, R2 and R3 running OSPF area 0 over 10G single-mode links. Last week a road-widening crew had a splice case relocated near mile marker 12 on the R1-R2 route, which is 18.4 km long. Since then, customers behind R2 have reported brief outages several times a day, mostly in the afternoon.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"R2 log (excerpt)", text:"Oct 26 13:14:51.220: %OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Process 10, Nbr 10.255.0.1 on TenGigabitEthernet0/0/0 from FULL to DOWN, Neighbor Down: Dead timer expired\nOct 26 13:14:53.884: %OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Process 10, Nbr 10.255.0.1 on TenGigabitEthernet0/0/0 from LOADING to FULL, Loading Done\nOct 26 15:02:07.031: %OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Process 10, Nbr 10.255.0.1 on TenGigabitEthernet0/0/0 from FULL to DOWN, Neighbor Down: Dead timer expired\nOct 26 15:02:09.640: %OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Process 10, Nbr 10.255.0.1 on TenGigabitEthernet0/0/0 from LOADING to FULL, Loading Done\nOct 26 16:41:33.402: %OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Process 10, Nbr 10.255.0.1 on TenGigabitEthernet0/0/0 from FULL to DOWN, Neighbor Down: Dead timer expired\nOct 26 16:41:36.118: %OSPF-5-ADJCHG: Process 10, Nbr 10.255.0.1 on TenGigabitEthernet0/0/0 from LOADING to FULL, Loading Done"},
     {label:"Interface counters on both ends of R1-R2", text:"R2# show interfaces Te0/0/0 | include line protocol|rate|errors|CRC\nTenGigabitEthernet0/0/0 is up, line protocol is up\n  5 minute input rate 2114000000 bits/sec, 241032 packets/sec\n  5 minute output rate 1987000000 bits/sec, 228810 packets/sec\n     48211 input errors, 48197 CRC, 0 frame, 0 overrun, 0 ignored\n     0 output errors, 0 collisions, 1 interface resets\n\nR1# show interfaces Te0/0/1 | include line protocol|errors|CRC\nTenGigabitEthernet0/0/1 is up, line protocol is up\n     12 input errors, 12 CRC, 0 frame, 0 overrun, 0 ignored\n     0 output errors, 0 collisions, 0 interface resets"},
     {label:"Transceiver DOM on both ends, plus commissioning baseline", text:"R2# show interfaces Te0/0/0 transceiver\nPort      Temp(C)  Voltage(V)  Current(mA)  Tx Power(dBm)  Rx Power(dBm)\nTe0/0/0   38.2     3.29        36.1         -0.6           -15.4\n  Rx thresholds: high alarm 0.5, low warning -14.9, low alarm -15.9\n\nR1# show interfaces Te0/0/1 transceiver\nPort      Temp(C)  Voltage(V)  Current(mA)  Tx Power(dBm)  Rx Power(dBm)\nTe0/0/1   36.9     3.30        35.8         0.5            -8.3\n\nCommissioning baseline (2025-03): R1 Rx -8.2 dBm, R2 Rx -8.1 dBm"},
     {label:"OTDR from the R1 hut, both fibers of the pair (1310/1550 nm, 100 ns pulse, IOR 1.4677)", text:"Fiber F07 (R1 Tx -> R2 Rx)\nEvent  Dist(km)  Loss1310(dB)  Loss1550(dB)  Refl(dB)  Type\n1      0.000     --            --            -55.0     Launch\n2      4.812     0.04          0.03          --        Splice\n3      9.507     0.06          0.05          --        Splice\n4      12.236    0.21          0.22          --        Splice\n5      12.251    7.05          9.60          --        Non-reflective\n6      18.402    --            --            -41.2     End (R2 patch panel)\n\nFiber F08 (R2 Tx -> R1 Rx)\nEvent  Dist(km)  Loss1310(dB)  Loss1550(dB)  Refl(dB)  Type\n1      0.000     --            --            -54.6     Launch\n2      4.812     0.03          0.03          --        Splice\n3      9.507     0.05          0.04          --        Splice\n4      12.236    0.04          0.03          --        Splice\n5      18.402    --            --            -40.8     End (R2 patch panel)"},
     {label:"R2 CPU history and a NOC note", text:"R2# show processes cpu history (summary)\n13:14  peak 64%  (top process: OSPF-10 Router, SPF run)\n15:02  peak 61%  (top process: OSPF-10 Router, SPF run)\n16:41  peak 63%  (top process: OSPF-10 Router, SPF run)\nall other intervals 6-11%\n\nNOC note: 'R2 CPU keeps spiking - is the route processor overloaded?'"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix09q1", type:"mc", q:"Which direction of the R1-R2 link is impaired?", options:["Both directions equally","R1 to R2 only. R2 counts tens of thousands of CRC errors and receives -15.4 dBm (baseline -8.1), while R1's receive side is clean and at baseline","R2 to R1 only","Neither: the optics are fine and R2's CPU is the problem"], answer:1, why:"Each direction uses its own fiber. Only R2's receiver, fed by F07 from R1's transmitter, shows low power and errors, so roughly 7.3 dB of new loss sits on F07."},
     {id:"ix09q2", type:"mc", q:"Why does OSPF report 'Dead timer expired' instead of the interface going down?", options:["The OSPF areas don't match","An MTU mismatch makes the database exchange fail","Light stays above loss of signal, so the link stays up. During error bursts, corrupted frames, R1's OSPF hellos among them, are dropped at the CRC check. When no hello arrives for 40 s, R2 drops the neighbor","Authentication keys rotated"], answer:2, why:"The adjacency comes back FULL within seconds, which rules out area, MTU and authentication mismatches; those stop it forming at all. At -15.4 dBm, between low warning and low alarm, the bit error rate climbs sharply with small changes such as afternoon heat."},
     {id:"ix09q3", type:"mc", q:"What does event 5 on fiber F07 indicate, and where is it?", options:["A macrobend about 15 m past the new splice at 12.236 km: 7.05 dB of non-reflective loss that is worse at 1550 nm, most likely fiber routed too tightly in, or at the entry of, the relocated splice case","A dirty connector at the R2 patch panel","The end of the fiber","An OTDR ghost from the launch reflection"], answer:0, why:"Non-reflective, higher at the longer wavelength, and present only on F07 in a case that F08 passes through cleanly: that is handling damage, typically a tight bend in the tray. The 0.21 dB splice just before it is also above a typical 0.1 dB spec."},
     {id:"ix09q4", type:"mc", q:"How should you read the CPU spikes on R2?", options:["As the root cause: upgrade R2's route processor","As a sign of a routing loop","As a reason to switch to static routes","As a consequence: each adjacency flap triggers an SPF recalculation, so the peaks at 13:14, 15:02 and 16:41 follow the flaps instead of causing them"], answer:3, why:"The peaks line up exactly with the ADJCHG messages, the top process is the OSPF SPF run, and the CPU sits at 6-11% the rest of the time. It's an effect, not a cause."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Uses counters and DOM from both ends to localize the fault to the R1→R2 fiber (CRC and low Rx only at R2, R1 clean), and compares against the commissioning baseline (about 7.3 dB of extra loss).","Explains the mechanism: marginal power raises the BER, frames fail the CRC, lost hellos let the dead timer expire, and the adjacency flaps without the link going down.","Reads the OTDR: a non-reflective event at 12.251 km, higher at 1550 nm, on F07 only, just past the new splice. That's a macrobend at the relocated splice case, and the 0.21 dB splice is above spec.","Plans the repair: dispatch to MM 12, re-dress F07 in the tray (re-splice if over about 0.1 dB), and meanwhile raise the OSPF cost on R1-R2 so traffic goes R1-R3-R2.","Verifies with a bidirectional OTDR, R2 Rx back near -8 dBm, cleared counters staying at 0 CRC, and no ADJCHG for days, and dismisses the CPU spikes as a consequence."],
     reference:"Both ends of the link tell the story. R2 has logged 48,197 CRC errors and receives -15.4 dBm against a commissioning baseline of -8.1 dBm, between its low-warning and low-alarm thresholds. R1 receives at baseline and has 12 CRCs. So only the R1→R2 fiber, F07, is damaged, with about 7.3 dB of new loss. At that level the link stays up, but the bit error rate rises steeply with small changes, such as afternoon heat in the splice case. Corrupted frames fail the CRC check and are dropped, OSPF hellos among them. When R2 hears no hello for the 40 s dead interval, it drops the adjacency, which comes back FULL seconds later. That is why customers see brief outages instead of a hard link-down. The OTDR places the fault. F07 has a 0.21 dB splice at 12.236 km and, 15 m further on, a 7.05 dB non-reflective loss that is worse at 1550 nm (9.60 dB). F08, through the same case, is clean. That's a macrobend, almost certainly F07 routed too tightly in the tray or at the cable entry during the relocation. The CPU peaks are SPF runs after each flap, an effect rather than the cause. Mitigate now by raising the OSPF cost on R1-R2 so traffic takes R1-R3-R2. Then dispatch to MM 12, re-dress F07, and re-splice if it's above about 0.1 dB. Verify with a bidirectional OTDR, R2 Rx back near -8 dBm, cleared counters staying at zero CRC, and no ADJCHG for several days.",
     alternatives:["If F07 can't be repaired quickly, move the R1-R2 circuit to a spare fiber in the same cable that tests clean on the OTDR, then repair F07 in a planned window.","Enable BFD on R1-R2 (with dampening) so OSPF fails over to R1-R3-R2 within milliseconds when errors burst, instead of after a 40 s dead interval, until the splice case is fixed."] },
   hints:["Compare the error counters and receive power at both ends. Is the problem in one direction or both?","Hellos are frames too. What happens to OSPF when frames keep failing the CRC check? Then compare the OTDR traces of F07 and F08 near 12.2 km.","A 7 dB non-reflective event, worse at 1550 nm, 15 m past the new splice on F07 only: a macrobend in the relocated splice case. Re-route traffic and re-dress the fiber."] },

 { id:"ix10", title:"Degraded mirror and a restore drill", level:3, domains:["p30","p1","p3","p15"],
   context:"Your small business's file server (Ubuntu with ZFS) keeps documents and job photos on a two-disk mirror pool called tank. A cron job takes nightly snapshots, and you've always called those 'the backups'. This morning ZED, the ZFS event daemon, emailed that the pool is DEGRADED. The box also looks short of memory, which worries you.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"zpool status -v tank", text:"  pool: tank\n state: DEGRADED\nstatus: One or more devices are faulted in response to persistent errors.\n\tSufficient replicas exist for the pool to continue functioning in a\n\tdegraded state.\naction: Replace the faulted device, or use 'zpool clear' to mark the device\n\trepaired.\n  scan: scrub repaired 1.2M in 05:41:17 with 0 errors on Sun Oct 25 06:05:18 2026\nconfig:\n\n\tNAME                                  STATE     READ WRITE CKSUM\n\ttank                                  DEGRADED     0     0     0\n\t  mirror-0                            DEGRADED     0     0     0\n\t    ata-WDC_WD40EFZX-68AWUN0_WD-WX12A  FAULTED     34     0    61  too many errors\n\t    ata-ST4000VN006-3CW104_ZW60B7QK   ONLINE       0     0     0\n\nerrors: No known data errors"},
     {label:"smartctl -A on both disks (selected attributes)", text:"$ sudo smartctl -A /dev/disk/by-id/ata-WDC_WD40EFZX-68AWUN0_WD-WX12A\nID# ATTRIBUTE_NAME          FLAG     VALUE WORST THRESH TYPE      UPDATED  WHEN_FAILED RAW_VALUE\n  5 Reallocated_Sector_Ct   0x0033   188   188   140    Pre-fail  Always       -       412\n  9 Power_On_Hours          0x0032   052   052   000    Old_age   Always       -       35110\n197 Current_Pending_Sector  0x0032   200   199   000    Old_age   Always       -       38\n198 Offline_Uncorrectable   0x0030   200   200   000    Old_age   Offline      -       21\n199 UDMA_CRC_Error_Count    0x0032   200   200   000    Old_age   Always       -       0\n\n$ sudo smartctl -A /dev/disk/by-id/ata-ST4000VN006-3CW104_ZW60B7QK   (RAW values)\n  5 Reallocated_Sector_Ct   0\n  9 Power_On_Hours          35098\n197 Current_Pending_Sector  0\n198 Offline_Uncorrectable   0\n199 UDMA_CRC_Error_Count    0"},
     {label:"Snapshots, the 'backup' cron job, and pool usage", text:"$ zfs list -t snapshot -o name,used,creation -r tank/docs | tail -3\ntank/docs@auto-2026-10-24  212M  Sat Oct 24  1:00 2026\ntank/docs@auto-2026-10-25  188M  Sun Oct 25  1:00 2026\ntank/docs@auto-2026-10-26  201M  Mon Oct 26  1:00 2026\n\n$ cat /etc/cron.d/zfs-backup\n# nightly backups\n0 1 * * * root /usr/sbin/zfs snapshot -r tank@auto-$(date +\\%F)\n\n$ zpool list tank\nNAME   SIZE  ALLOC   FREE  CKPOINT  EXPANDSZ   FRAG    CAP  DEDUP    HEALTH  ALTROOT\ntank  3.62T  2.31T  1.31T        -         -    18%    63%  1.00x  DEGRADED  -"},
     {label:"Memory (the worry)", text:"$ free -h\n               total        used        free      shared  buff/cache   available\nMem:            31Gi        27Gi       1.1Gi        12Mi       2.9Gi       3.6Gi\nSwap:          2.0Gi          0B       2.0Gi\n\n$ arc_summary | grep -A3 'ARC size (current)'\nARC size (current):                                    77.4 %   23.9 GiB\n        Target size (adaptive):                        77.6 %   24.0 GiB\n        Min size (hard limit):                          6.2 %    1.9 GiB\n        Max size (high water):                           16:1   30.9 GiB"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix10q1", type:"mc", q:"What does zpool status tell you about the data right now?", options:["Data has been lost and must be restored from snapshots","Both disks are fine. Run zpool clear and carry on","The pool is running on the Seagate alone. There are no known data errors, but no redundancy is left, so one unreadable sector or a failure on that disk now means data loss","The pool is read-only until the disk is replaced"], answer:2, why:"'No known data errors' means every block could still be read correctly from the good side of the mirror. DEGRADED means the pool still works but has no redundancy left."},
     {id:"ix10q2", type:"mc", q:"What do the SMART attributes say about the WD disk?", options:["Its media is failing: 412 reallocated sectors, 38 pending and 21 uncorrectable. A UDMA CRC count of 0 rules out the cable, so replace the disk","It's a loose SATA cable, so reseat it","It's a firmware bug and the disk is fine","The disk is simply old and these numbers are normal for its age"], answer:0, why:"Reallocated, pending and uncorrectable sectors are media defects that tend to grow. Cable faults show up as UDMA CRC errors, and that count is 0. The Seagate has the same power-on hours and no defects, so age alone doesn't explain it."},
     {id:"ix10q3", type:"mc", q:"Are the nightly snapshots a backup?", options:["Yes: snapshots are point-in-time copies","Yes, as long as more than 7 of them are kept","No, because snapshots can't be restored file by file","No: they live inside tank. If the pool is lost (a second disk failure, a controller fault, fire, or an accidental zpool destroy), the snapshots go with it"], answer:3, why:"Snapshots protect against deletion and overwrite mistakes. They are not a separate copy on separate media. You can restore single files from them (via .zfs/snapshot), which is useful, but that doesn't make them a backup."},
     {id:"ix10q4", type:"mc", q:"free shows 27 GiB 'used' and 1.1 GiB free. What should you do about memory?", options:["Add RAM before the resilver","Nothing urgent. The ARC (23.9 GiB) is ZFS's read cache and gives memory back under pressure, and Linux reports it as 'used', not 'buff/cache'","Set zfs_arc_max to 2 GiB right away","Reboot to clear a memory leak"], answer:1, why:"The ARC is designed to use spare RAM and shrink when applications need it. Capping it hard would only slow the resilver and file access. It's a red herring here."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Interprets DEGRADED correctly (no data errors yet, but zero redundancy), identifies the WD as failing media from SMART (reallocated, pending, uncorrectable, CRC 0), and notes both disks are the same age.","Protects the data before stressing the survivor: makes an immediate off-pool copy (zfs send of the latest snapshot to an external disk or another host) before or during the resilver.","Replaces the disk properly: zpool replace with /dev/disk/by-id paths, monitors the resilver, scrubs afterwards, and confirms ONLINE with 0 errors.","Explains why snapshots on the same pool aren't backups, and sets up real ones (replication to separate media or a separate host with zfs send/receive or syncoid, plus an offsite copy).","Does a restore drill (restores sample files from the off-pool copy, compares checksums, times it), and dismisses the ARC memory worry with evidence."],
     reference:"The pool still works and reports no data errors, but it has no redundancy left, so everything now depends on the Seagate. The WD's SMART data shows failing media: 412 reallocated sectors, 38 pending and 21 offline-uncorrectable. Its UDMA CRC count is 0, so this isn't a cable problem, and the 34 read and 61 checksum errors in zpool status follow from the bad sectors. Both disks have about 35,100 power-on hours, so the survivor is the same age, and the resilver will read it hard. Order matters. First, get an off-pool copy of the most important data: plug in a USB disk, create a pool on it, and zfs send tank/docs@auto-2026-10-26 (then the photos) to it. The nightly snapshots are not backups. They live in the same pool, so a second disk failure, a controller fault or an accidental destroy would take them too. Next, run zpool replace tank with the WD's by-id name and the new disk's by-id name, watch the resilver in zpool status, then scrub and confirm both disks are ONLINE with 0 errors. After that, make backups real: nightly zfs send/receive (for example with syncoid) to a second machine or external pool, plus an offsite copy with retention. Then run a restore drill. Restore a folder and some photos from the backup into a scratch directory, compare sha256sums with the originals, and record how long it took. The memory 'shortage' is just the ARC, ZFS's read cache, which shrinks when applications need RAM.",
     alternatives:["If no spare disk is on hand, attach a USB disk to the healthy side (zpool attach) to get redundancy back while the replacement ships, then replace and detach properly.","Send the data to cloud object storage with restic or rclone for an immediate offsite copy, and add local replication next."] },
   hints:["DEGRADED doesn't mean data loss. Ask what would happen if the remaining disk had a problem today.","Compare the WD's SMART counters with the Seagate's, and ask where the snapshots physically live.","The WD's media is failing and the snapshots are on the same pool. Copy the data off-pool first, then zpool replace, scrub, and run a test restore."] },
 { id:"ix11", title:"Web server won't start after a port change", level:3, domains:["p29","p3","p5","p2"],
   context:"On web03, a RHEL 9 VM, you moved the internal crew portal in nginx from port 80 to 8090 so that a new reverse proxy on another host can take over port 80. After you edited /etc/nginx/conf.d/portal.conf and ran systemctl restart nginx, nginx won't start. A colleague says: 'Just run setenforce 0. SELinux always breaks things.'",
   evidence:[
     {label:"systemctl status nginx (excerpt)", text:"× nginx.service - The nginx HTTP and reverse proxy server\n     Loaded: loaded (/usr/lib/systemd/system/nginx.service; enabled; preset: disabled)\n     Active: failed (Result: exit-code) since Mon 2026-10-26 14:20:31 CDT; 9s ago\n    Process: 4410 ExecStartPre=/usr/bin/rm -f /run/nginx.pid (code=exited, status=0/SUCCESS)\n    Process: 4411 ExecStartPre=/usr/sbin/nginx -t (code=exited, status=1/FAILURE)\n\nOct 26 14:20:31 web03 nginx[4411]: nginx: the configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf syntax is ok\nOct 26 14:20:31 web03 nginx[4411]: nginx: [emerg] bind() to 0.0.0.0:8090 failed (13: Permission denied)\nOct 26 14:20:31 web03 nginx[4411]: nginx: configuration file /etc/nginx/nginx.conf test failed\nOct 26 14:20:31 web03 systemd[1]: nginx.service: Control process exited, code=exited, status=1/FAILURE\nOct 26 14:20:31 web03 systemd[1]: nginx.service: Failed with result 'exit-code'.\nOct 26 14:20:31 web03 systemd[1]: Failed to start The nginx HTTP and reverse proxy server."},
     {label:"Audit log, SELinux mode, port labels, listeners and file label", text:"# ausearch -m AVC -ts recent\n----\ntime->Mon Oct 26 14:20:31 2026\ntype=PROCTITLE msg=audit(1793042431.512:612): proctitle=2F7573722F7362696E2F6E67696E78002D74\ntype=SYSCALL msg=audit(1793042431.512:612): arch=c000003e syscall=49 success=no exit=-13 a0=6 a1=55d0c2a1b2e0 a2=10 a3=7ffd4c2e1a3c items=0 ppid=1 pid=4411 auid=4294967295 uid=0 gid=0 euid=0 suid=0 fsuid=0 egid=0 sgid=0 fsgid=0 tty=(none) ses=4294967295 comm=\"nginx\" exe=\"/usr/sbin/nginx\" subj=system_u:system_r:httpd_t:s0 key=(null)\ntype=AVC msg=audit(1793042431.512:612): avc:  denied  { name_bind } for  pid=4411 comm=\"nginx\" src=8090 scontext=system_u:system_r:httpd_t:s0 tcontext=system_u:object_r:unreserved_port_t:s0 tclass=tcp_socket permissive=0\n\n# getenforce\nEnforcing\n# semanage port -l | grep -w http_port_t\nhttp_port_t                    tcp      80, 81, 443, 488, 8008, 8009, 8443, 9000\n# ss -tlnp | grep -E ':(80|8090) '\n(no output)\n# ls -lZ /etc/nginx/conf.d/portal.conf\n-rw-r--r--. 1 root root system_u:object_r:httpd_config_t:s0 412 Oct 26 14:18 /etc/nginx/conf.d/portal.conf"},
     {label:"firewall-cmd --list-all", text:"public (active)\n  target: default\n  icmp-block-inversion: no\n  interfaces: ens192\n  sources:\n  services: cockpit dhcpv6-client http ssh\n  ports:\n  protocols:\n  forward: yes\n  masquerade: no"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix11q1", type:"mc", q:"What does '(13: Permission denied)' mean in this case?", options:["portal.conf has the wrong file permissions","Only root may bind ports above 1024","SELinux denies the httpd_t domain from binding a port labeled unreserved_port_t (the AVC name_bind denial)","Another process is already listening on 8090"], answer:2, why:"The AVC from the same second shows the name_bind denial for src=8090, and 8090 isn't in http_port_t. The file is readable and labeled httpd_config_t. Ports above 1024 don't need root, and the bind runs as uid 0 anyway. A port already in use returns errno 98 (EADDRINUSE), not 13, and ss shows nothing on 8090."},
     {id:"ix11q2", type:"mc", q:"What is the right fix with SELinux kept enforcing?", options:["setenforce 0","semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8090","chcon -t bin_t /usr/sbin/nginx","setsebool -P httpd_can_network_connect 1"], answer:1, why:"Labeling the port tells the policy that web servers may bind 8090, and the label persists across reboots and relabels. setenforce 0 removes protection from the whole machine. Relabeling the binary breaks its domain transition. httpd_can_network_connect governs outbound connections from httpd, not binding."},
     {id:"ix11q3", type:"mc", q:"nginx now starts and curl http://localhost:8090 works on web03, but curl http://web03.example.com:8090 from your laptop times out. What next?", options:["Open the port in firewalld: firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=8090/tcp, then firewall-cmd --reload","Restart nginx again","Add another SELinux port label","Flush the laptop's DNS cache"], answer:0, why:"The public zone allows only cockpit, dhcpv6-client, http (80) and ssh. A timeout, rather than 'connection refused', from outside while localhost works is the classic sign of a host firewall dropping packets. Once port 80 is no longer used, remove the http service too."},
     {id:"ix11q4", type:"mc", q:"How do you confirm the fix is complete and will survive a reboot?", options:["Check that the nginx service is enabled","Run nginx -t once more","Disable SELinux briefly to make sure it's no longer involved","Confirm getenforce says Enforcing, semanage lists 8090 under http_port_t, firewall-cmd --list-ports (runtime and --permanent) shows 8090/tcp, ss shows nginx on 8090, a remote curl works and ausearch shows no new AVCs. Then restart or reboot to prove it persists"], answer:3, why:"A complete check covers every layer that blocked the service (SELinux label, firewall, the listener itself) and proves persistence, not just 'it works right now'. Turning SELinux off would prove nothing about the fix."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Reads the failure precisely: bind() fails with EACCES (13) during the nginx -t pre-start check, matched to the AVC name_bind denial for src=8090 with tcontext unreserved_port_t.","Rules out the other explanations with evidence: the file is labeled httpd_config_t and readable, ss shows no listener (a port in use would be errno 98), and the bind runs as root.","Fixes it the right way, with semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8090, keeps SELinux enforcing, and explains why setenforce 0 and httpd_can_network_connect are wrong.","Finds the second layer: firewalld allows http/80 but not 8090/tcp. Opens 8090 permanently and removes the unused http service.","Verifies end to end (remote curl, no new AVCs, label and rule survive a restart or reboot) and documents the change."],
     reference:"nginx's syntax is fine, but its pre-start test dies at bind() with errno 13, EACCES. On RHEL that usually means SELinux, and the audit log proves it. At the same second there's an AVC denying name_bind for comm=nginx, src=8090, from httpd_t to unreserved_port_t. The targeted policy lets web servers bind only ports labeled http_port_t (80, 81, 443, 488, 8008, 8009, 8443, 9000), and 8090 isn't one of them. The other theories don't fit. portal.conf is readable and labeled httpd_config_t. Nothing is listening on 8090, and a busy port would give errno 98, not 13. The bind runs as root. setenforce 0 would 'work' only by removing protection from the whole server. The precise fix is to label the port: semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8090, then systemctl restart nginx. httpd_can_network_connect is a distractor, since it covers outbound connections. Once nginx is running, test from another machine. firewalld's public zone allows http (80) but not 8090, so remote clients will time out until you run firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=8090/tcp and firewall-cmd --reload. Remove the http service once nothing uses port 80. To verify: getenforce shows Enforcing, ss shows nginx on 8090, curl from the laptop returns the portal, ausearch -m AVC -ts recent shows nothing new, and both changes survive a reboot.",
     alternatives:["Pick a port that is already labeled http_port_t, such as 8443 or 8008, so no SELinux change is needed. It still has to be opened in firewalld.","Pipe the denial through audit2why (ausearch -m AVC -ts recent | audit2why) to get the explanation and the suggested semanage command, and apply that specific fix instead of building a broad audit2allow module."] },
   hints:["Errno 13 from bind() on RHEL: what besides file permissions can deny it? Check the audit log.","Compare the AVC's tcontext with the ports listed for http_port_t. After nginx starts, test from another machine, not just localhost.","Run semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 8090 and keep SELinux enforcing, then open 8090/tcp in firewalld."] },

 { id:"ix12", title:"Scanners freeze while pickers walk", level:3, domains:["p10","p42","p2","p28"],
   context:"A distribution warehouse uses 2.4 GHz-only handheld barcode scanners on the WPA2-Enterprise SSID WH-SCAN. Last month the APs were re-provisioned by an Ansible playbook that set every radio to maximum transmit power 'to improve coverage', and two new APs went in at aisles 18 and 22. Since then, pickers in aisles 14-20 complain that scanners freeze for 5-15 seconds while they walk, then recover.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"AP inventory (controller export)", text:"AP      Location      Band    Channel  Tx power  Clients  Ch. util\nAP-01   Dock east     2.4GHz  1        20 dBm    14       38%\nAP-02   Aisle 4       2.4GHz  6        20 dBm    9        41%\nAP-03   Aisle 10      2.4GHz  11       20 dBm    6        35%\nAP-04   Aisle 14      2.4GHz  1        20 dBm    31       62%\nAP-05   Aisle 18      2.4GHz  6        20 dBm    2        71%\nAP-06   Aisle 22      2.4GHz  6        20 dBm    3        71%\nAP-07   Mezzanine     2.4GHz  11       20 dBm    4        33%"},
     {label:"Controller event log for scanner SCN-117", text:"14:02:11 SCN-117 a4:5e:60:12:7c:01 assoc AP-04 ch1 RSSI -51 dBm rate 54 Mbps\n14:04:38 SCN-117 on AP-04 RSSI -74 dBm rate 5.5 Mbps retries 38%\n14:04:52 SCN-117 on AP-04 RSSI -79 dBm rate 1 Mbps retries 61%\n14:05:03 SCN-117 deauth from AP-04 reason 4 (inactivity)\n14:05:09 SCN-117 assoc AP-05 ch6 RSSI -55 dBm\n14:05:10 SCN-117 802.1X: EAP-PEAP full authentication started (RADIUS 10.40.0.20)\n14:05:14 SCN-117 802.1X: EAP success, 4-way handshake complete\n14:05:15 SCN-117 DHCP renew OK 10.40.8.117 (lease 8h)\n\nDHCP scope 10.40.8.0/22: 312 of 1018 addresses in use (31%)"},
     {label:"Walk survey at aisle 18 (what the scanner hears), plus the WLAN profile", text:"Aisle 18 midpoint, scanner's view:\n  AP-04 ch1  -68 dBm\n  AP-05 ch6  -54 dBm\n  AP-06 ch6  -58 dBm\nScanner transmit power (spec sheet): 13 dBm max\nScanner roam trigger (vendor default): current AP below -70 dBm\n\nWLAN WH-SCAN:\n  Security: WPA2-Enterprise (802.1X, PEAP)\n  802.11r (FT): disabled   OKC: disabled   802.11k: disabled   802.11v: disabled\n  Basic (mandatory) rates: 1, 2, 5.5, 11 Mbps"},
     {label:"Ansible source of truth and a dry run", text:"# inventory/group_vars/warehouse_aps.yml\nradio_24ghz:\n  tx_power_dbm: max\n  min_basic_rate_mbps: 1\nwlan_wh_scan:\n  fast_transition: false\n  okc: false\nap_channel_map:\n  AP-04: 1\n  AP-05: 6\n  AP-06: 6     # copied from AP-05 when added\n\n$ ansible-playbook site.yml --limit wlc01 --check --diff | tail -2\nPLAY RECAP *********************************************************************\nwlc01 : ok=14 changed=0 unreachable=0 failed=0 skipped=0 rescued=0 ignored=0"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix12q1", type:"mc", q:"Why does SCN-117 freeze?", options:["The DHCP scope is running out of addresses","It clings to AP-04 well past the point of usefulness (a sticky client) while its frames fail (61% retries at 1 Mbps). When it finally moves, it has to do a full 802.1X/PEAP authentication, about 5 s, because fast roaming is off","AP-05 is overloaded with clients","A microwave oven in the break room"], answer:1, why:"From 14:04:38 to 14:05:15 the scanner is first barely usable on AP-04, then disconnected, then authenticating. DHCP took one second and the scope is 31% used. AP-05 has two clients."},
     {id:"ix12q2", type:"mc", q:"The scanner hears AP-04 at -68 dBm, but AP-04 hears the scanner at -79 dBm. Why does that matter?", options:["It doesn't: RSSI readings are always noisy","The 11 dB gap proves the scanner's antenna is broken","It means AP-04 should raise its power even further","The AP transmits at 20 dBm and the scanner at only 13 dBm. The scanner still hears AP-04 above its -70 dBm roam trigger, so it doesn't roam, while the AP can barely hear it. Max AP power creates this link imbalance and keeps clients sticky"], answer:3, why:"Roaming is the client's decision, and it's based on what the client hears. With AP power well above client power, the downlink looks fine long after the uplink has failed. Matching AP power to client power makes the client roam while both directions still work."},
     {id:"ix12q3", type:"mc", q:"What is wrong with the channel plan in aisles 14-22?", options:["AP-05 and AP-06 are neighbors on the same channel 6 (copied when AP-06 was added), so they share one channel's airtime (71% utilization on both). With 1/6/11, neighboring APs should be on different channels","They should use channels 1, 2 and 3","2.4 GHz should use 40 MHz channels for more capacity","Every AP should be on channel 1 so clients roam more easily"], answer:0, why:"Two APs on the same channel within earshot of each other take turns on the air, so both report the same high utilization. Channels 1, 2 and 3 overlap each other, and 40 MHz on 2.4 GHz leaves no clean channels."},
     {id:"ix12q4", type:"mc", q:"How should the configuration fix be made?", options:["Change power and channels by hand in the controller GUI","Add more APs at maximum power","In the Ansible source of truth: set power to roughly match the clients (about 11-14 dBm), raise the minimum basic rate (for example to 12 Mbps), fix AP-06 in the channel map, enable 802.11r/OKC and 11k/v if the scanners support them, run --check --diff, apply, then re-survey","Switch the SSID to WPA2-Personal so roaming is faster"], answer:2, why:"The dry run shows the controller matches the playbook, so a hand edit would be reverted on the next run. Higher minimum rates stop clients clinging to distant APs. More max-power APs worsen co-channel interference, and dropping 802.1X trades away per-user authentication when fast roaming would solve the delay."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Reads the client log as a sticky-client roam: it stays on AP-04 down to -79 dBm with 61% retries, then a full 802.1X authentication takes about 5 s with fast roaming disabled, which accounts for the freeze.","Explains the power imbalance (AP 20 dBm vs scanner 13 dBm, -68 vs -79 dBm) and why max power and 1 Mbps basic rates keep clients attached too long.","Spots the co-channel problem: AP-05 and AP-06 are neighbors on channel 6 with 71% utilization, because of a copied channel-map entry.","Makes the fix in the Ansible variables, the source of truth (power, minimum rates, channel map, 802.11r/k/v where supported), and previews it with --check --diff so the change isn't silently reverted.","Verifies with a walk test and the controller log (roams under about 100 ms with FT or OKC, retries below about 15%, lower channel utilization), and dismisses DHCP with evidence."],
     reference:"The freeze is a slow roam, not a coverage hole. SCN-117 joins AP-04 in aisle 14 and keeps using it as the picker walks away. By 14:04:52 the AP hears it at -79 dBm with 61% retries at 1 Mbps, and almost nothing gets through. The scanner only moves when AP-04 deauthenticates it at 14:05:03. Because 802.11r and OKC are off, it then does a full PEAP authentication against RADIUS and is usable again at 14:05:15. That's about 12-15 seconds, matching the complaints. The playbook's max-power setting causes the stickiness. APs transmit at 20 dBm and scanners at 13 dBm, so in aisle 18 the scanner still hears AP-04 at -68 dBm, above its -70 dBm roam trigger, while AP-04 barely hears the scanner. Basic rates down to 1 Mbps let that weak link limp along. AP-06 also copied AP-05's channel 6, so those two neighbors share airtime at 71% utilization. DHCP is a red herring: the renewal took a second and the scope is 31% used. Fix it in the source of truth, or the next playbook run will undo it. In group_vars, set 2.4 GHz power to about 11-14 dBm, raise the minimum basic rate to 12 Mbps, and re-plan 1/6/11 so neighbors differ (for example, AP-06 to 11). Enable 802.11r and k/v if the scanner firmware supports them, OKC if not. Run --check --diff and apply. Then walk aisles 14-22 with a scanner and check roam times and retries in the controller log.",
     alternatives:["If the scanners can't do 802.11r, enable OKC (PMK caching) on the controller and in the scanner profile to skip the full EAP exchange on most roams, and raise the scanner's roam trigger (many enterprise handhelds expose it) to about -65 dBm.","Plan to replace the scanners with dual-band models that support 5 GHz and 802.11k/v/r, which gives many more non-overlapping channels, and keep 2.4 GHz for legacy devices."] },
   hints:["Follow SCN-117 through the controller log minute by minute. Where do the seconds actually go?","Compare what the scanner hears from AP-04 with what AP-04 hears from the scanner, and check which channels AP-05 and AP-06 use.","Max AP power makes clients sticky, AP-06 copied channel 6, and roaming does a full 802.1X exchange. Lower the power, raise minimum rates, fix the channel and enable fast roaming, all in the Ansible vars."] },

 { id:"ix13", title:"Monday logon failures on the Macs", level:4, domains:["p16","p30","p2","p43"],
   context:"Over the weekend the office's only domain controller, DC01 (Windows Server 2022, PDC emulator for corp.example.com), was live-migrated to a new Hyper-V host, HV02. On Monday morning every Mac bound to AD fails network logon and can't mount the SMB shares. The Windows PCs log on normally, but several users mention their taskbar clocks look about eight minutes fast.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"A Mac (Terminal), run at the same moment as the DC01 commands below", text:"$ kinit jlopez@CORP.EXAMPLE.COM\njlopez@CORP.EXAMPLE.COM's password:\nkinit: krb5_get_init_creds: Clock skew too great\n\n$ date\nMon Oct 26 08:33:40 CDT 2026\n$ sntp time.apple.com\n+0.012345 +/- 0.021034 time.apple.com 198.51.100.17"},
     {label:"DC01: w32tm status and a check against an independent NTP server", text:"C:\\> w32tm /query /status\nLeap Indicator: 0(no warning)\nStratum: 2 (secondary reference - syncd by (S)NTP)\nPrecision: -23 (119.209ns per tick)\nRoot Delay: 0.0000000s\nRoot Dispersion: 10.0000000s\nReferenceId: 0x564D4943 (source name:  \"VMIC\")\nLast Successful Sync Time: 10/26/2026 8:40:02 AM\nSource: VM IC Time Synchronization Provider\nPoll Interval: 6 (64s)\n\nC:\\> w32tm /stripchart /computer:time.example.net /samples:2 /dataonly\nTracking time.example.net [203.0.113.123:123].\nCollecting 2 samples.\nThe current time is 10/26/2026 8:41:22 AM.\n08:41:22, -462.1183247s\n08:41:24, -462.1181960s"},
     {label:"Hyper-V host HV02 and a Windows client", text:"PS C:\\> w32tm /query /source\nLocal CMOS Clock\nPS C:\\> w32tm /stripchart /computer:time.example.net /samples:1 /dataonly\nTracking time.example.net [203.0.113.123:123].\nCollecting 1 samples.\nThe current time is 10/26/2026 8:41:25 AM.\n08:41:25, -461.9821142s\nPS C:\\> Get-VMIntegrationService -VMName DC01 -Name 'Time Synchronization'\n\nVMName Name                 Enabled PrimaryStatusDescription SecondaryStatusDescription\n------ ----                 ------- ------------------------ --------------------------\nDC01   Time Synchronization True    OK\n\nPC-114> w32tm /query /source\nDC01.corp.example.com"},
     {label:"DC01 event logs (excerpt)", text:"Security    4771  Kerberos pre-authentication failed.\n                  Account Name: jlopez   Client Address: ::ffff:192.168.50.61\n                  Failure Code: 0x25\n(same event for 11 other Mac users since 07:45)\n\nDNS Server  4013  The DNS server is waiting for Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) to signal that the initial synchronization of the directory has been completed.\n                  (logged once, Sat 23:12, at boot after the migration)"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix13q1", type:"mc", q:"Why do the Macs fail while the Windows PCs log on?", options:["The Macs don't support AES Kerberos encryption types","The Macs' AD binding broke during the migration","Domain-joined Windows PCs take their time from the DC, so they share its error and stay within 5 minutes of it. The Macs sync with Apple's servers, so they're about 7.7 minutes away from the DC, beyond Kerberos's default 5-minute tolerance","DNS event 4013 means AD isn't running"], answer:2, why:"Failure code 0x25 is KRB_AP_ERR_SKEW, which kinit reports as 'Clock skew too great'. The 'fast' taskbar clocks show the Windows PCs have followed the DC's wrong time. Encryption-type or binding problems would show different errors and wouldn't line up with time."},
     {id:"ix13q2", type:"mc", q:"Where is DC01's wrong time coming from?", options:["From time.example.net, which is wrong","From the Hyper-V Time Synchronization integration service. DC01's source is 'VM IC Time Synchronization Provider', and the new host HV02 runs on its own unsynchronized CMOS clock, about 462 s fast","From the Macs","From a daylight-saving rule change"], answer:1, why:"Both DC01 and HV02 measure about -462 s against an independent NTP server, the Macs agree with Apple's servers, and DC01's ReferenceId is VMIC. The bad time starts at the host and flows down through the DC to every domain member."},
     {id:"ix13q3", type:"mc", q:"Which fix is correct?", options:["Raise the Kerberos maximum clock skew to 15 minutes in the Default Domain Policy","Unbind and rebind every Mac","Set each Mac's clock to match the DC by hand","Make DC01, the PDC emulator, the authoritative time source: disable the VMIC time provider on DC01 (or Hyper-V time sync for that VM, per your standard), configure a reliable peer (w32tm /config /manualpeerlist:time.example.net,0x8 /syncfromflags:manual /reliable:yes /update), resync, and give HV02 a proper time source too"], answer:3, why:"The forest's time should flow from the PDC emulator, which syncs from a reliable external source. Widening the skew tolerance weakens replay protection and leaves every clock wrong. Hand-setting the Macs would break them against the real world."},
     {id:"ix13q4", type:"mc", q:"How should you treat DNS event 4013 on DC01?", options:["As expected noise. A DC logs 4013 at boot while it waits for initial AD sync, this one was logged once on Saturday night, and Windows logons work today","As the cause: AD is down, so Kerberos fails","As a sign that DNS needs reinstalling","As a reason to add a second DNS server to the Macs"], answer:0, why:"4013 is common on a single-DC domain after a reboot and clears once AD DS is ready. Windows users authenticating fine proves AD and DNS are up. It's the red herring."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Connects the symptoms: the Macs get KRB_AP_ERR_SKEW (4771 code 0x25, 'Clock skew too great'), and the Windows PCs work because they follow the DC's wrong time (their clocks are fast).","Measures the skew against an independent reference (stripchart about -462 s on DC01 and HV02, Macs synced to Apple) instead of trusting any one clock.","Traces the cause to virtualization: DC01's time source is the VMIC provider, and HV02 runs on an unsynced CMOS clock after the migration.","Applies the correct time hierarchy: the PDC emulator syncs from a reliable external source with the VMIC provider disabled for that VM, HV02 gets a proper source, then w32tm /resync. Does not raise the Kerberos skew tolerance.","Verifies (DC01's source shows the external peer with an offset under 1 s, the Macs can kinit and mount shares, Windows clocks converge) and treats 4013 as a red herring."],
     reference:"This is a time problem, and Kerberos is the victim. Kerberos rejects requests when the client and KDC clocks differ by more than 5 minutes. The Mac's kinit says 'Clock skew too great', and DC01 logs 4771 with failure code 0x25 (KRB_AP_ERR_SKEW). The question is which clock is wrong, and an independent reference answers it. From DC01, w32tm /stripchart against time.example.net shows -462 s: the DC is about 7 minutes 42 seconds fast. The Macs sync with Apple's servers and are correct. Domain-joined Windows PCs take their time from the DC, so they share its error, which is why their logons work and their clocks look fast. DC01's source is 'VM IC Time Synchronization Provider', meaning it takes time from its Hyper-V host. HV02, new this weekend, runs on its own CMOS clock with no NTP source and is also 462 s fast. The migration put a bad host clock under the one machine every other clock trusts. Fix the hierarchy. On DC01, the PDC emulator, disable the VMICTimeProvider (or the VM's time-sync integration, per your standard). Run w32tm /config /manualpeerlist:time.example.net,0x8 /syncfromflags:manual /reliable:yes /update, then w32tm /resync /rediscover. Give HV02 a proper source too. Don't raise the Kerberos skew tolerance. Verify that DC01's source shows the external peer, stripchart reads within a second, the Macs can kinit and mount shares, and Windows clocks converge. Event 4013 is a normal boot-time message.",
     alternatives:["As a fast stopgap, correct HV02's clock first. DC01 follows its host through the VMIC provider within minutes, so the Macs work again. Then fix the DC's hierarchy properly so it no longer depends on the host.","If your standard keeps Hyper-V time sync enabled, make sure every host syncs from the domain hierarchy or a reliable NTP source, and add 'host time verified' to the DC migration checklist."] },
   hints:["Kerberos is very sensitive to one thing that has nothing to do with passwords. Which devices have the right time, and which don't?","Measure each machine against an independent NTP server, and look at where DC01 gets its time from.","DC01 takes time from the new Hyper-V host (VMIC), which is 462 s fast. Make the PDC emulator sync from a reliable external source and fix HV02's clock."] },

 { id:"ix14", title:"A deploy that took down the database", level:4, domains:["p20","p19","p5","p18","p37"],
   context:"At 13:05 the team merged PR #482, 'Normalize cache keys', and CD rolled it out to all six web pods within a minute. By 13:06 product-page p95 latency had gone from 80 ms to 9 s, PostgreSQL hit 100% CPU, and the site served 503s for 14 minutes until someone rolled back. Every CI check on the PR was green. You're writing the incident analysis.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"git show for the merged commit", text:"$ git show --stat 3f9c2ab\n    PR #482: Normalize cache keys\n app/cache.py        | 2 +-\n requirements.txt    | 2 +-\n tests/test_cache.py | 3 ++-\n$ git show 3f9c2ab -- app/cache.py requirements.txt\n@@ app/cache.py @@\n CACHE_TTL = 3600  # seconds\n def product_key(product_id, locale):\n-    return f\"prod:{product_id}:{locale}\"\n+    return f\"prod:v2:{product_id}:{locale.lower()}\"\n def get_product(product_id, locale):\n     key = product_key(product_id, locale); data = cache.get(key)\n     if data is None:\n         data = db.load_product(product_id, locale)   # 6 queries, ~40 ms on an idle DB\n         cache.set(key, data, ex=CACHE_TTL)\n     return data\n@@ requirements.txt @@\n-requests==2.32.3   # payments client only\n+requests==2.32.4   # payments client only"},
     {label:"Dashboard (1-minute samples)", text:"time   cache_hit  db_conns(max 100)  db_cpu  p95_ms  http_503/min\n13:04  0.97       12                 22%     81      0\n13:06  0.08       100                100%    9120    412\n13:08  0.21       100                100%    8010    401\n13:12  0.38       100                100%    6480    322\n13:16  0.52       98                 96%     4210    207\n13:19  -- rollback to previous image --\n13:21  0.81       41                 58%     240     12\n13:25  0.96       13                 23%     84      0"},
     {label:"pg_stat_statements: incident window (13:05-13:19) vs a normal 14 minutes", text:"query                                               calls(incident)  calls(normal)  mean_ms(incident)\nSELECT ... FROM products WHERE id = $1              412,880          13,950         2,840\nSELECT ... FROM product_variants WHERE product_id   409,115          13,870         3,120\nSELECT ... FROM product_images WHERE product_id     405,770          13,820         1,960\n(3 more product-page queries with the same ~30x ratio)\n\ndistinct product_id values loaded in the window: 17,940  (about 23 loads per product)"},
     {label:"Redis INFO at 13:10", text:"used_memory_human:3.12G\nmaxmemory_human:5.00G\nevicted_keys:0\nkeyspace_hits:1204551\nkeyspace_misses:2911870\nconnected_clients:212"},
     {label:"CI workflow, rollout strategy and the updated unit test", text:"# .github/workflows/ci.yml (excerpt)\njobs:\n  test:\n    steps:\n      - run: pytest -q tests/unit\n  deploy:\n    needs: test\n    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    steps:\n      - run: kubectl set image deployment/web web=registry.example.com/web:${{ github.sha }}\n# k8s/web-deployment.yaml (excerpt)\nstrategy:\n  type: RollingUpdate\n  rollingUpdate:\n    maxSurge: 100%\n    maxUnavailable: 50%\n# tests/test_cache.py\ndef test_product_key():\n    assert product_key(42, \"en-US\") == \"prod:v2:42:en-us\""}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix14q1", type:"mc", q:"What caused the outage?", options:["The requests 2.32.3 → 2.32.4 bump in the same PR","Redis evicting keys under memory pressure","Changing the key format (prod:v2:...) made every existing cache entry unreachable at once. The hit ratio fell from 97% to 8%, and every page view went to Postgres, saturating its 100 connections and its CPU","Kubernetes nodes running out of CPU during the rollout"], answer:2, why:"The collapse matches the rollout to the minute. Redis shows evicted_keys:0 with memory to spare, so the misses are lookups for keys that don't exist yet. requests is used only by the payments client, which the product page doesn't call."},
     {id:"ix14q2", type:"mc", q:"pg_stat_statements shows about 23 loads per product during the incident. What does that tell you?", options:["The ORM is issuing N+1 queries","There's no request coalescing. When a popular product's key is missing, every concurrent request for it misses and runs the same load, so a slow, saturated DB gets the same work dozens of times: a cache stampede, or dogpile","The query planner chose bad plans","Some products have 23 variants each"], answer:1, why:"Normally each product loads about once per TTL. During the incident, each one loaded about 23 times because hundreds of requests missed together and nothing let one of them fill the cache while the others waited. Each load also took about 3 s instead of 40 ms, which widened the window."},
     {id:"ix14q3", type:"mc", q:"Which set of changes best prevents a repeat?", options:["Migrate keys safely (read v2 and fall back to v1, then backfill; or pre-warm v2 before switching), add per-key request coalescing (single-flight or a short lock) plus stale-while-revalidate, add TTL jitter, and roll out through a canary gated on cache hit ratio and DB load","Remove the cache and buy a bigger database","Raise CACHE_TTL to one day","Forbid changes to cache.py"], answer:0, why:"The fix addresses each link in the chain: the cold start (migration with fallback or pre-warming), the amplification (coalescing), synchronized expiry later (jitter), and blast radius (canary). A bigger DB or a longer TTL doesn't stop the next cold start."},
     {id:"ix14q4", type:"mc", q:"The unit test passed. What test or gate was missing?", options:["A test asserting the exact key string, which already exists","A linter rule for f-strings","A stricter type check on locale","A check of behaviour under production-like load: a one-pod canary stage that rolls back automatically when cache hit ratio, DB connections or p95 cross thresholds, or a pre-deploy load test against a warm cache"], answer:3, why:"The change was functionally correct, so a unit test can't catch it. The regression was operational (capacity under a cold cache), and only a canary or load test would have shown it, with the damage limited to one pod's share of traffic."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Ties the outage to the key-format change: every v1 entry became unreachable, so the hit ratio fell from 0.97 to 0.08 at 13:06, and Postgres connections and CPU saturated.","Explains the stampede (no coalescing, about 23 loads per product, slow loads under contention) and why recovery was slow, and dismisses the requests bump and Redis evictions with evidence.","Explains why the rollout made it worse (maxSurge 100%, maxUnavailable 50%: the whole fleet switched at once, with no canary) and why green unit tests couldn't catch an operational regression.","Proposes a safe key migration (dual-read with fallback, or pre-warming) plus single-flight or locking, stale-while-revalidate and TTL jitter, and a canary stage with SLO gates and automatic rollback.","Says how to verify: re-ship the change through the canary and watch hit ratio, DB connections and p95, add a cold-cache load test to the pipeline, and record actions in a blameless postmortem."],
     reference:"The one-line change to product_key (adding v2 and lower-casing the locale) renamed every key in the cache. Correctness didn't change, so the unit test passed, but at 13:06 every pod was asking for keys that didn't exist yet. The hit ratio fell from 0.97 to 0.08, and nearly every product view ran the 6-query load against Postgres. The DB hit its 100-connection limit and 100% CPU, loads that take 40 ms on an idle DB took about 3 s, and requests queued into 503s. Nothing coalesced the misses either. pg_stat_statements shows about 23 loads per product, because every concurrent miss for a hot key ran the same load. That's a classic cache stampede, and a saturated DB refills the cache slowly, so recovery crawled. Rolling back worked quickly because the v1 keys, with a one-hour TTL, were mostly still in Redis. The requests bump only touches the payments client, and Redis shows evicted_keys:0, so both are red herrings. The rollout amplified the damage: maxSurge 100% and maxUnavailable 50% switched the whole fleet at once, and CI only ran unit tests. To prevent a repeat, migrate keys deliberately, either reading v2 with fallback to v1 and backfilling, or pre-warming v2 with jittered TTLs. Add single-flight or a short per-key lock with stale-while-revalidate. Deploy through a one-pod canary that rolls back automatically on hit ratio, DB connections or p95. Verify by re-shipping the change through that canary and watching those graphs.",
     alternatives:["Make key-schema changes backward compatible by policy (expand and contract): ship a reader for both versions, then the writer, then remove v1, each in its own deploy.","Put a connection pooler (PgBouncer) and a concurrency limit in front of the DB, so a cold cache degrades to slower pages instead of exhausting connections. That limits the blast radius but doesn't replace the migration plan."] },
   hints:["Line up the merge time with the cache hit ratio. What happened to the entries already in Redis?","Look at loads per product in pg_stat_statements and at the rollout strategy. Why did the whole fleet miss at once, and why so many times per key?","The new key prefix made the warm cache invisible, causing a stampede with no coalescing. Migrate keys with fallback or pre-warming, add single-flight and jitter, and gate the rollout behind a canary."] },

 { id:"ix15", title:"CrashLoopBackOff after scaling up", level:4, domains:["p19","p18","p14","p20"],
   context:"Before a product launch, someone raised the orders-api Deployment from 4 to 12 replicas. Now five pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, the Running ones are slow, and the database team says Postgres is 'full'. Each pod runs gunicorn with 4 workers, and each worker creates its own SQLAlchemy engine and connection pool.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"kubectl get pods -l app=orders-api", text:"NAME                          READY   STATUS             RESTARTS       AGE\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-2xkqp   1/1     Running            0              41m\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-4hzt8   1/1     Running            0              41m\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-5mcdw   1/1     Running            0              41m\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-7vqmn   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   6 (2m11s ago)  14m\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-8kq2r   1/1     Running            0              41m\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-b9xtl   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   6 (98s ago)    14m\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-dq6pv   1/1     Running            1 (12m ago)    14m\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-g7f4n   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   5 (3m40s ago)  14m\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-jw2hs   1/1     Running            0              14m\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-p4z8m   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   6 (61s ago)    14m\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-tt5nk   1/1     Running            2 (9m ago)     14m\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-zz81q   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   6 (2m50s ago)  14m"},
     {label:"Previous container log, pod state, and memory use", text:"$ kubectl logs orders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-7vqmn --previous | tail -5\n[2026-10-26 15:02:44 +0000] [9] [INFO] Booting worker with pid: 9\nsqlalchemy.exc.OperationalError: (psycopg2.OperationalError) connection to server at \"pg-primary.db.svc.cluster.local\" (10.20.3.15), port 5432 failed: FATAL:  sorry, too many clients already\n[2026-10-26 15:02:45 +0000] [7] [ERROR] Worker (pid:9) exited with code 3\n[2026-10-26 15:02:45 +0000] [7] [ERROR] Shutting down: Master\n[2026-10-26 15:02:45 +0000] [7] [ERROR] Reason: Worker failed to boot.\n\n$ kubectl describe pod orders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-7vqmn | grep -A3 'Last State'\n    Last State:     Terminated\n      Reason:       Error\n      Exit Code:    3\n      Started:      Mon, 26 Oct 2026 15:02:41 +0000\n\n$ kubectl top pod orders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-2xkqp\nNAME                          CPU(cores)   MEMORY(bytes)\norders-api-6d9c7b9f6b-2xkqp   180m         184Mi          (limit 512Mi)"},
     {label:"App, gunicorn and autoscaler config", text:"# app/db.py\nengine = create_engine(DB_URL, pool_size=10, max_overflow=5, pool_pre_ping=True)\nwith engine.connect() as c:          # startup check: schema version\n    c.execute(text(\"SELECT version FROM schema_migrations\"))\n\n# gunicorn.conf.py\nworkers = 4\npreload_app = False\n\n# hpa/orders-api.yaml\nminReplicas: 12\nmaxReplicas: 20"},
     {label:"PostgreSQL", text:"postgres=# SHOW max_connections;\n max_connections\n-----------------\n 200\n\npostgres=# SHOW superuser_reserved_connections;\n superuser_reserved_connections\n--------------------------------\n 3\n\npostgres=# SELECT usename, application_name, state, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity\n           WHERE backend_type = 'client backend' GROUP BY 1,2,3 ORDER BY 4 DESC;\n  usename   | application_name | state  | count\n------------+------------------+--------+-------\n orders_app | orders-api       | idle   |   175\n orders_app | orders-api       | active |    13\n reporting  | metabase         | idle   |     9\n postgres   | psql             | active |     1"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix15q1", type:"mc", q:"Why are five pods in CrashLoopBackOff?", options:["They're OOMKilled at the 512Mi limit","Each new pod's gunicorn workers connect at startup, Postgres refuses them ('sorry, too many clients already'), the worker exits with code 3, gunicorn's master shuts down, and the kubelet restarts the container with growing back-off","The image can't be pulled from the registry","The liveness probe path is wrong"], answer:1, why:"The previous container's log shows the FATAL from Postgres and 'Worker failed to boot'. describe shows Reason: Error with exit code 3, not OOMKilled (exit 137), and kubectl top shows 184Mi of 512Mi."},
     {id:"ix15q2", type:"mc", q:"How many connections can the Deployment demand at 12 replicas, in the worst case?", options:["48","120","480","720"], answer:3, why:"Each worker has its own pool: pool_size 10 + max_overflow 5 = 15. 4 workers × 15 = 60 per pod, and × 12 pods = 720, against 197 slots for normal users (200 minus 3 reserved). Even the original 4 replicas could demand 240."},
     {id:"ix15q3", type:"mc", q:"175 orders-api connections are idle. Why does that matter?", options:["It doesn't: idle connections use no resources","It proves Postgres is slow","Each pool keeps its connections open while idle, so the slots are stranded in the existing pods' pools and new pods can't get any. Capacity is fragmented, not used up by real work: only 13 connections are active","It shows the app has a connection leak"], answer:2, why:"Pools keep connections ready to reuse, which is efficient for one process but wasteful across 48 processes. The DB is turning clients away while doing almost nothing. Each idle backend also holds memory on the server."},
     {id:"ix15q4", type:"mc", q:"Which plan fixes this durably?", options:["Put PgBouncer in transaction pooling mode between the app and Postgres (or size the pools so replicas × workers × (pool_size + max_overflow) fits the budget with headroom), cap HPA maxReplicas to that budget, and make startup retry with back-off instead of crashing","Raise max_connections to 2000","Drop to 4 replicas permanently","Set pool_pre_ping=False to save connections"], answer:0, why:"A pooler decouples the number of app connections from server backends, and a budget makes scaling decisions explicit. Thousands of Postgres backends cost memory and contention. Four replicas would block the launch. pre_ping doesn't open extra connections."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Reads the crash correctly: 'too many clients already' at worker boot, exit code 3, then CrashLoopBackOff. Rules out OOM using the exit code and kubectl top.","Computes the connection demand, 12 × 4 × (10 + 5) = 720, against 197 usable slots, and notes that even the old 4 replicas could demand 240.","Explains why the Running pods are slow and why idle connections stranded in per-worker pools fragment capacity (175 idle vs 13 active).","Gives a durable design: PgBouncer transaction pooling and/or a pool budget, HPA bounds tied to that budget, startup retries with back-off, and a readiness probe that reflects DB health. Explains why a much higher max_connections is costly.","Says how to verify: a load test at the target replica count, pg_stat_activity staying under budget, zero restarts, p95 latency, and an alert on connection usage."],
     reference:"The pods aren't crashing on their own. Postgres is refusing them. Each gunicorn worker opens a connection at startup to check the schema version. In the new pods that fails with 'FATAL: sorry, too many clients already', so the worker exits with code 3, gunicorn's master gives up ('Worker failed to boot'), and the kubelet restarts the container with growing back-off: CrashLoopBackOff. describe shows exit code 3, not OOMKilled, and kubectl top shows 184Mi of 512Mi. The arithmetic explains it. Each worker has its own pool of up to 15 connections (pool_size 10 + max_overflow 5), so a pod can hold 60, and 12 replicas can demand 720. Postgres has 200 slots, 3 of them reserved for superusers, and Metabase holds 9. pg_stat_activity shows only 13 orders-api connections doing work while 175 sit idle in pools. The older pods' pools are hoarding slots, so new pods get none, and the Running pods queue for their own small share, which is why they're slow. The design flaw is per-process pools multiplied by replicas with no budget. The durable fix is PgBouncer in transaction mode in front of Postgres, so hundreds of client connections share about 40-60 server connections. Alternatively, set a budget, for example 150 connections for orders-api across 12 pods × 4 workers, about 3 each (pool_size 2, max_overflow 1), and cap the HPA's maxReplicas to match. Make startup retry with back-off. Raising max_connections to 1000 costs memory per backend. Verify with a load test at the target replica count.",
     alternatives:["Switch gunicorn to fewer processes with threads (or an async worker) and one shared pool per process, cutting the connection count while keeping throughput, then redo the budget arithmetic.","As an immediate mitigation, scale back to a replica count the DB can serve (or lower max_overflow through config) to stop the crash loop, then roll out PgBouncer before the launch."] },
   hints:["Read the previous container's log and the exit code. Is this memory, or something the app depends on?","Multiply it out: replicas × workers × (pool_size + max_overflow). Compare that with max_connections, and look at idle versus active connections.","Per-worker pools at 12 replicas can demand 720 connections against about 197 slots. Add PgBouncer or set a pool budget, and cap replicas to match."] },
 { id:"ix16", title:"The NAT gateway line item", level:4, domains:["p17","p2","p19","p14"],
   context:"Your team's AWS bill in us-east-1 jumped from about $1,900 in September to about $6,400 in October. Early in October you moved the CI runners and the nightly log-processing job from public subnets into private subnets 'for security', with NAT gateways for outbound access. Finance wants to know what happened and what to do about it.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"Cost Explorer, usage types for us-east-1", text:"Usage type                         Sep usage      Sep cost   Oct usage      Oct cost\nUSE1-NatGateway-Bytes              1,210 GB       $54.45     96,850 GB      $4,358.25\nUSE1-NatGateway-Hours              1,440 hrs      $64.80     1,488 hrs      $66.96\nUSE1-BoxUsage:c6i.2xlarge          2,160 hrs      $734.40    2,232 hrs      $758.88\nUSE1-TimedStorage-ByteHrs (S3)     39,600 GB-Mo   $910.80    41,200 GB-Mo   $947.60\nUSE1-DataTransfer-Regional-Bytes   2,050 GB       $20.50     2,140 GB       $21.40\n(rates: NAT $0.045/hour and $0.045/GB processed; S3 Standard $0.023/GB-month)\n\nFinance comment: 'S3 is our second-biggest line. Should we move the logs to Glacier?'"},
     {label:"VPC Flow Logs through the NAT gateways in October (Athena, joined with AWS ip-ranges), and workload notes", text:"destination                          GB        share\nS3 (us-east-1)                       76,300    78.8%\nregistry-1.docker.io (Docker Hub)    17,900    18.5%\nECR API (us-east-1)                   1,250     1.3%\nother internet                        1,400     1.4%\ntotal                                96,850\n\nNightly log job (AWS Batch on c6i.2xlarge, private subnets): reads ~2.4 TB/night of raw logs from s3://example-raw-logs (us-east-1) and writes ~60 GB of Parquet back.\nCI runners (private subnets): ~1,070 jobs/day; each job does a fresh docker pull of 3 base images (~540 MB total) from Docker Hub; no layer cache is kept between jobs."},
     {label:"Private subnet route table and VPC endpoints", text:"$ aws ec2 describe-route-tables --route-table-ids rtb-0priv1234 --query 'RouteTables[].Routes'\n[\n  [\n    { \"DestinationCidrBlock\": \"10.40.0.0/16\", \"GatewayId\": \"local\", \"State\": \"active\" },\n    { \"DestinationCidrBlock\": \"0.0.0.0/0\", \"NatGatewayId\": \"nat-0f1e2d3c4b5a69788\", \"State\": \"active\" }\n  ]\n]\n$ aws ec2 describe-vpc-endpoints --filters Name=vpc-id,Values=vpc-0abc1234 --query 'VpcEndpoints[].ServiceName'\n[]"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix16q1", type:"mc", q:"What drove the increase?", options:["S3 storage growth","NAT gateway data processing: 96,850 GB at $0.045/GB = $4,358, up from $54. Most of it is S3 traffic (76,300 GB) and Docker Hub pulls (17,900 GB) that started going through NAT when the workloads moved to private subnets","More EC2 instance hours","Inter-AZ data transfer"], answer:1, why:"Line by line, NatGateway-Bytes rose by about $4,300 of the roughly $4,500 increase. S3 storage rose 4%, EC2 hours 3%, and regional transfer is flat."},
     {id:"ix16q2", type:"mc", q:"What is the cheapest fix for the S3 portion?", options:["An S3 interface endpoint (PrivateLink) in each AZ","Move the batch instances back to public subnets with public IPs","Compress the logs before reading them","Add an S3 gateway VPC endpoint and associate it with the private subnets' route tables. S3 gateway endpoints have no hourly or per-GB charge, and same-region S3 traffic stops going through NAT"], answer:3, why:"A gateway endpoint adds a prefix-list route (pl-...) that is more specific than 0.0.0.0/0, so S3 traffic bypasses the NAT and stays on the AWS network. Interface endpoints work but charge per hour and per GB. Going back to public subnets undoes the security change, and the job has to read what is stored."},
     {id:"ix16q3", type:"mc", q:"Roughly how much per month does the S3 gateway endpoint save, at October's volume?", options:["About $3,430","About $340","About $34,300","About $760"], answer:0, why:"76,300 GB × $0.045/GB ≈ $3,434. Data transfer between S3 and EC2 in the same region is free, so the NAT processing charge was the entire cost of that traffic."},
     {id:"ix16q4", type:"mc", q:"What should be done about the Docker Hub traffic?", options:["Nothing: image pulls are free","Move all CI back to the public subnets","Cache the images: an ECR pull-through cache rule for Docker Hub (or a registry mirror) plus a shared layer cache on the runners, so each base image crosses the NAT about once instead of about 1,070 times a day. ECR serves layers from S3, through the new gateway endpoint","Switch to a bigger NAT gateway"], answer:2, why:"17,900 GB × $0.045 ≈ $806 a month for downloading the same three images over and over. Caching also avoids Docker Hub rate limits and speeds up jobs. NAT gateways scale automatically, and the charge is per GB, so size isn't the issue."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Reads the bill as a NAT data-processing problem: NatGateway-Bytes went from 1,210 GB to 96,850 GB ($54 to $4,358), and the timing matches the move to private subnets.","Uses the flow logs to attribute the bytes (S3 76,300 GB, Docker Hub 17,900 GB) and checks them against the workloads (2.4 TB of log reads a night, about 1,070 uncached CI pulls a day).","Explains the network path: the private route table sends 0.0.0.0/0 to NAT and there are no VPC endpoints, so even same-region S3 traffic pays NAT processing.","Fixes it without undoing the security goal: an S3 gateway endpoint on the private route tables, plus an ECR pull-through cache or mirror and runner layer caching, with the savings worked out (about $3,430 + $800 a month).","Verifies the fix (a pl- route in the route table, daily NAT bytes falling in Cost Explorer, jobs still succeeding), adds a budget or anomaly alert, and dismisses S3 storage and EC2 as red herrings."],
     reference:"The bill rose about $4,500, and almost all of it is one line: NatGateway-Bytes went from 1,210 GB ($54) to 96,850 GB ($4,358). Storage, compute and NAT hours barely moved. S3 storage is a big line but rose only 4%, so moving logs to Glacier wouldn't touch this. A NAT gateway charges $0.045 for every GB it processes, whatever the destination. When the batch job and CI runners moved into private subnets, the route table sent everything outside 10.40.0.0/16 to the NAT gateway. With no VPC endpoints, that included traffic to S3 in the same region. The flow logs confirm it. 76,300 GB went to S3, matching 2.4 TB of log reads a night for 31 days, and 17,900 GB went to Docker Hub, matching about 1,070 uncached 540 MB pulls a day. Fix the S3 part with a gateway VPC endpoint for S3 on the private route tables. It's free, it adds a prefix-list route more specific than 0.0.0.0/0, and the traffic stays private, so the security goal survives. That saves about 76,300 × $0.045 ≈ $3,430 a month. For CI, add an ECR pull-through cache rule for Docker Hub and a layer cache on the runners. Layers then come from ECR via S3 through the same endpoint, which saves most of the roughly $800 a month and avoids Docker Hub rate limits. Verify with the route table (a pl- route), daily NAT bytes in Cost Explorer and successful jobs, then add a Cost Anomaly Detection alert on NAT.",
     alternatives:["Process the logs where they already live (for example with Athena, or an EMR or Batch job in a VPC with endpoints), so 2.4 TB a night never crosses a NAT.","If the runners need broad internet access anyway, keep NAT for that, but also add interface endpoints for ECR (api and dkr) and STS, and compare their hourly plus $0.01/GB cost against NAT processing at your volume."] },
   hints:["Compare September and October line by line. Which single usage type explains almost all of the increase?","Where does traffic from a private subnet go when the only default route points at a NAT gateway, even when the destination is S3 in the same region?","With no VPC endpoints, 76 TB of S3 traffic paid NAT processing. Add an S3 gateway endpoint to the private route tables and cache the CI images."] },

 { id:"ix17", title:"Training throughput halved overnight", level:4, domains:["p9","p1","p38","p35"],
   context:"A workstation with two 24 GB GPUs, in a small office server closet, fine-tunes an image-classification model every night. Since facilities changed the building HVAC to switch off at 20:00, the job's throughput falls from about 410 to about 210 images/s partway through the run, and the run no longer finishes before staff arrive at 07:00. Nothing changed in the code or data, and the MLOps dashboard still shows GPU utilization at 99-100%.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"Training log (excerpt)", text:"20:30 start: 12 epochs x 960 steps, batch 1024 images/step\n21:10 [epoch 2/12]  step 960   imgs/s 412.6  data_wait 3.1%  loss 0.412\n22:40 [epoch 4/12]  step 3100  imgs/s 396.0  data_wait 2.9%  loss 0.351\n23:50 [epoch 5/12]  step 4620  imgs/s 338.4  data_wait 2.4%  loss 0.336\n00:20 [epoch 6/12]  step 5150  imgs/s 271.3  data_wait 1.9%  loss 0.322\n02:40 [epoch 8/12]  step 7080  imgs/s 214.9  data_wait 1.4%  loss 0.288\n04:55 [epoch 10/12] step 8750  imgs/s 209.7  data_wait 1.4%  loss 0.271\n      (at this rate the remaining 2,770 steps finish at about 08:40)"},
     {label:"nvidia-smi dmon -s pucv -d 60 (selected rows)", text:"# gpu   pwr gtemp mtemp    sm   mem   enc   dec  mclk  pclk pviol tviol\n# Idx     W     C     C     %     %     %     %   MHz   MHz     %     %\n    0   412    71     -    99    61     0     0 10501  2610     0     0    <- 21:10\n    1   405    73     -    99    60     0     0 10501  2595     0     0    <- 21:10\n    0   236    88     -   100    34     0     0 10501  1245     0   100    <- 02:40\n    1   229    89     -   100    33     0     0 10501  1230     0   100    <- 02:40"},
     {label:"nvidia-smi -q -d PERFORMANCE,TEMPERATURE for GPU 1 at 02:40 (excerpt)", text:"    Clocks Event Reasons\n        Idle                              : Not Active\n        Applications Clocks Setting       : Not Active\n        SW Power Cap                      : Not Active\n        HW Slowdown                       : Not Active\n            HW Thermal Slowdown           : Not Active\n            HW Power Brake Slowdown       : Not Active\n        Sync Boost                        : Not Active\n        SW Thermal Slowdown               : Active\n        Display Clock Setting             : Not Active\n    Temperature\n        GPU Current Temp                  : 89 C\n        GPU Shutdown Temp                 : 95 C\n        GPU Slowdown Temp                 : 92 C\n        GPU Max Operating Temp            : 88 C\n(fan speed reported separately: 100 %)"},
     {label:"Closet air temperature and a PCIe link check", text:"Closet air:  19:30 23.1 C | 21:00 26.4 C | 23:00 31.8 C | 01:00 35.2 C | 03:00 36.0 C | 06:30 35.7 C\n\n$ nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,pcie.link.gen.current,pcie.link.width.current --format=csv\nindex, pcie.link.gen.current, pcie.link.width.current\n0, 4, 16\n1, 4, 8\n\nBuild sheet (2025-11): 'GPU1 slot runs x8 when both slots are populated (board lane split).'"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix17q1", type:"mc", q:"The dashboard shows 99-100% GPU utilization during the slow period. Why doesn't that mean the GPUs are running at full speed?", options:["The dashboard is broken","'Utilization' is the fraction of time a kernel was running, not how much work got done. At 1,230-1,245 MHz instead of about 2,600 MHz, the same kernels take about twice as long while utilization stays near 100%","Utilization only counts memory, not compute","Utilization is averaged across both GPUs"], answer:1, why:"nvidia-smi's utilization is a busy-time metric. A GPU at half clock speed is still 'busy' all the time, just doing half as much per second. Throughput (images/s) and clock speed are the numbers that show the drop."},
     {id:"ix17q2", type:"mc", q:"Which evidence identifies the cause?", options:["data_wait falling from 3.1% to 1.4%","GPU1's x8 PCIe link","SW Thermal Slowdown active, tviol at 100%, both GPUs at 88-89 C (at the 88 C max operating temperature) with clocks halved, and closet air rising from 23 C to 36 C after the HVAC switches off at 20:00","The memory clock staying at 10,501 MHz"], answer:2, why:"The throttle reason, the temperature against its limit, the clock drop and the room-temperature curve all line up in time. data_wait falls because the GPU got slower, so it waits on data less. That's a consequence."},
     {id:"ix17q3", type:"mc", q:"Why does throughput fall roughly in proportion to the core clock?", options:["The job is compute-bound on the GPU (low data_wait, SMs always busy), so work per second scales with clock frequency: about 2,600 → 1,240 MHz roughly halves images/s (412 → 210)","Lower GPU clocks cause more CPU cache misses","The memory clock dropped as well","The OS scheduler deprioritizes hot GPUs"], answer:0, why:"When the bottleneck is arithmetic in the SMs, time per step is roughly cycles needed ÷ clock rate. 1,240/2,600 ≈ 0.48 and 210/412 ≈ 0.51. The memory clock didn't change, which is why the drop is slightly less than proportional for memory-heavy kernels."},
     {id:"ix17q4", type:"mc", q:"What is the best plan?", options:["Increase DataLoader num_workers","Move GPU1 to a x16 slot","Upgrade PyTorch","Fix the airflow (an exhaust fan for the closet or an HVAC exception, clean filters, make sure hot exhaust isn't recirculated to the intake). Meanwhile cap GPU power (for example nvidia-smi -pl 300), which usually costs much less speed than throttling does, and add alerts on clocks, throttle reasons and images/s"], answer:3, why:"The cause is heat, so the fix is cooling. A power cap makes the slowdown predictable and smaller in the meantime. The data loader isn't the bottleneck, and GPU1 has always been x8, while both GPUs slowed equally."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Shows that 'utilization' isn't throughput: the SMs are busy 100% of the time, but at half the clock.","Pins the cause on the throttle reasons (SW Thermal Slowdown active, tviol 100%), temperatures at their limits and the clock drop, correlated with closet temperature after the HVAC schedule change.","Explains why throughput scales with clock for a compute-bound job (low data_wait), using the numbers (about 2,600 → 1,240 MHz, 412 → 210 imgs/s).","Dismisses the red herrings: the x8 PCIe link has been there since the build and both GPUs slowed equally, and the data loader isn't the bottleneck.","Proposes cooling fixes plus a power cap as mitigation and MLOps monitoring (export clocks and throttle reasons, alert on images/s), and verifies with a full night's run showing steady clocks and completion before 07:00."],
     reference:"The GPUs are fully busy, just slower. GPU 'utilization' only measures the fraction of time a kernel was running. At 21:10 both GPUs run at about 2,600 MHz and the job does 412 images/s. By 02:40 they're at 1,230-1,245 MHz, still showing 100% utilization, and the job does about 210 images/s. Throughput fell almost exactly in proportion to the clock, which is what a compute-bound job does, and data_wait is only 1-3%, so the input pipeline isn't the limit. nvidia-smi says why the clocks dropped. SW Thermal Slowdown is Active, tviol is 100%, and both GPUs sit at 88-89 C, their maximum operating temperature, with fans at 100%. The driver is cutting clocks to hold temperature. The closet sensor explains the timing: the air climbs from 23 C to 36 C after the HVAC switches off at 20:00, so the coolers are pulling in hot air. GPU1's x8 link is a red herring. It has been x8 since the build, and both GPUs slowed equally. Fix the airflow first, with an HVAC exception for the closet or an exhaust fan venting to a cooler space, and clean the filters. Meanwhile, cap power with nvidia-smi -pl 300 on each GPU. Less power means less heat, and the speed lost is usually far less than throttling costs. Export clocks, temperatures and throttle reasons to the MLOps dashboard and alert on images/s. Verify with a full night's run: steady clocks, no thermal slowdown, and the job finished well before 07:00.",
     alternatives:["Start the run earlier, while the HVAC is still on, and checkpoint so the job can pause through the hottest hours. That takes longer in calendar time but gives steady throughput.","Move the workstation out of the closet into a cooled room or a rack with front-to-back airflow, and add inlet temperature to the training job's pre-flight health checks."] },
   hints:["Utilization says the GPU is busy. Does it say how fast it's working? Compare the clocks at 21:10 and 02:40.","Read the Clocks Event Reasons and the temperature limits, then line them up with the closet's air temperature through the night.","SW Thermal Slowdown at 88-89 C halves the clock after the HVAC switches off. Fix the airflow, cap power in the meantime, and alert on clocks and images/s."] },

 { id:"ix18", title:"Temperature probe jumps when the pump runs", level:4, domains:["p23","p10","p14","p22"],
   context:"At a pump station, a Raspberry Pi with an ADS1115 ADC reads a 4-20 mA temperature transmitter (0-100 °C) across a 150 Ω shunt resistor. Operators see the tank temperature jump by about 2 °C every time the pump motor starts, and drop back when it stops. A handheld thermometer in the tank shows no change. You have a month of logged data and a multimeter.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"pandas summary of a month of logged data (1 sample per second)", text:">>> df.groupby('pump_on')['temp_c'].describe()\n           count   mean   std    min    25%    50%    75%    max\npump_on\nFalse    41210.0  41.82  0.21  41.31  41.66  41.81  41.97  42.38\nTrue     28644.0  43.87  0.24  43.20  43.71  43.86  44.04  44.61\n\n>>> step = df.temp_c.diff()[df.pump_on.astype(int).diff() == 1]\n>>> step.describe()[['count', 'mean', 'std']]\ncount    37.000\nmean      2.041\nstd       0.090\n# every step completes within a single 1 s sample"},
     {label:"Multimeter readings, pump off vs pump on", text:"Measurement                                      Pump off    Pump on\nLoop current (meter in series)                   10.69 mA    10.69 mA\nVoltage directly across the shunt                1.6035 V    1.6035 V\nPi GND to shunt low side (loop supply 0 V)       0.4 mV      48.7 mV\nADS1115 reading, AIN0 vs GND (from the logger)   1.6039 V    1.6522 V\nPi 5 V rail at the GPIO header                   5.08 V      5.07 V"},
     {label:"Wiring notes, the reading code, and dmesg on the Pi", text:"- Pi powered from a 5 V DIN-rail PSU; its 0 V is bonded to the panel ground bar.\n- 24 V loop supply 0 V is bonded at the pump motor's ground lug on the far side of the panel.\n- Sensor cable shield grounded at both ends (panel and transmitter head).\n- ADS1115 wired single-ended: AIN0 = shunt high side; ADC GND = Pi GND; AIN1 unused.\n\n# read_temp.py\nchan = AnalogIn(ads, ADS.P0)            # single-ended\ntemp_c = (chan.voltage / 150.0 * 1000 - 4.0) / 16.0 * 100.0\n\n$ dmesg | grep -i voltage\n[    4.812331] hwmon hwmon1: Undervoltage detected!\n[    9.118904] hwmon hwmon1: Voltage normalised\n(no further undervoltage messages in 31 days of uptime; 37 pump starts logged)"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix18q1", type:"mc", q:"Is the tank temperature actually changing when the pump starts?", options:["No. The loop current (10.69 mA) and the voltage across the shunt are identical with the pump on and off, so the transmitter's output, and therefore the temperature it reports, doesn't change. The error is in how the Pi measures it","Yes: the pump stirs in warmer water","Yes, but only near the sensor","It can't be determined without a second sensor"], answer:0, why:"The transmitter turns temperature into current, and the current is unchanged. Also, a 2 °C step that completes within one 1 s sample is physically implausible for a tank of water, and the handheld agrees."},
     {id:"ix18q2", type:"mc", q:"What voltage error explains the 2.0 °C jump?", options:["About 4.8 mV","About 0.48 V","About 48 mV: 2.04 °C is 0.33 mA on the 16 mA span, and 0.33 mA × 150 Ω ≈ 49 mV, matching the 48.7 mV measured between Pi GND and the shunt's low side","About 2 V"], answer:2, why:"2.04 °C / 100 °C × 16 mA = 0.326 mA, and 0.326 mA × 150 Ω = 48.9 mV. The ADC's own readings differ by 1.6522 - 1.6039 = 48.3 mV. Three independent numbers agree."},
     {id:"ix18q3", type:"mc", q:"What is the mechanism?", options:["The ADC reference drifts when the Pi is undervolted","The single-ended input measures AIN0 against the Pi's ground, not against the shunt's low side. The two grounds are bonded at different points, and when the motor runs, its current through the shared ground path lifts the loop's 0 V about 48 mV above the Pi's. That ground-loop voltage adds straight onto the reading","The pump's magnetic field changes the shunt's resistance","The transmitter heats itself when the pump runs"], answer:1, why:"A single-ended reading is (shunt voltage) + (offset between the two grounds). The shunt voltage is constant, and the offset appears only when motor current flows. The shield grounded at both ends adds another loop path."},
     {id:"ix18q4", type:"mc", q:"Which fix addresses the cause?", options:["Average more samples to smooth out the jump","Subtract 2.04 °C in software whenever pump_on is true","Replace the Pi's power supply","Measure differentially across the shunt (AIN0-AIN1, with AIN1 on the shunt's low side) and/or remove the ground loop: one ground reference point, the shield grounded at one end only, or a loop isolator. Then re-run the step analysis to confirm the jump is gone"], answer:3, why:"A differential measurement rejects the common-mode ground offset (48 mV is well inside the ADS1115's input range), and fixing the grounding removes it at the source. A software offset breaks whenever motor load changes, and the PSU's undervoltage happened once, at boot."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Proves the process isn't changing: loop current and shunt voltage are identical pump on and off, the handheld agrees, and a 2 °C step within one 1 s sample is physically implausible.","Does the arithmetic: 2.04 °C → 0.33 mA → about 49 mV on 150 Ω, matching the 48.7 mV ground offset and the ADC delta (1.6522 - 1.6039 V).","Explains the ground loop: single-ended measurement referenced to Pi GND, separate bonding points, motor current in the shared ground, and the shield grounded at both ends.","Fixes the cause (differential measurement across the shunt, single-point grounding, shield grounded at one end, or a loop isolator), not with a software offset, and dismisses the boot-time undervoltage message.","Verifies with data: after the fix the pump-on and pump-off means agree within noise and the mean step is about 0, plus a spot check against the handheld."],
     reference:"The tank isn't changing temperature. The measurement is. With the pump on and off, the meter shows the same loop current (10.69 mA, which is 41.8 °C) and the same voltage across the shunt, and the handheld agrees. Besides, no tank of water warms 2 °C within one 1-second sample. What does change is the voltage from the Pi's ground to the shunt's low side: 0.4 mV pump off, 48.7 mV pump on. Work it backwards: 2.04 °C is 2.04% of the 16 mA span, about 0.33 mA, and 0.33 mA × 150 Ω ≈ 49 mV, close to the ADC's own 48.3 mV delta. The cause is a ground loop. The ADS1115 is wired single-ended, so it measures AIN0 against the Pi's ground, not across the shunt. The Pi's 0 V is bonded at the panel ground bar and the loop supply's 0 V at the motor's ground lug, and the shield is grounded at both ends. Motor current through the shared ground path lifts the loop's 0 V about 48 mV above the Pi's, and the reading inherits that offset. Fix it in hardware: wire AIN1 to the shunt's low side and read differentially with AnalogIn(ads, ADS.P0, ADS.P1). Ground the shield at the panel end only, and use one bonding point or a loop isolator. Don't subtract 2 °C in software, and ignore the unrelated boot-time undervoltage message. To verify, rerun the groupby and step analysis: the means should agree and the step should be about 0.",
     alternatives:["Fit a loop-powered 4-20 mA signal isolator between the transmitter loop and the ADC input. It breaks the galvanic path entirely and is standard practice near motors and VFDs.","Use an isolated ADC module with its own isolated supply, so the Pi's ground and the motor's ground are never connected through the measurement."] },
   hints:["Before blaming the sensor, check whether the transmitter's output actually changes. What does the loop current do when the pump starts?","Convert 2 °C into milliamps, then into volts across 150 Ω. Which multimeter reading matches that number?","The ADC measures single-ended against a ground that sits 48 mV away from the shunt's low side while the motor runs. Measure differentially across the shunt and break the ground loop."] },

 { id:"ix19", title:"Local LLM slows as conversations grow", level:5, domains:["p12","p9","p34","p14","p38"],
   context:"You serve an 8-billion-parameter model (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head dimension 128, FP16 weights and KV cache) with vLLM on one 24 GB GPU for an internal RAG assistant that answers questions from the fiber-installation manuals. It was fast in testing. Now, with 10-15 simultaneous users, long chat histories and 12 retrieved chunks per question, time-to-first-token (TTFT) climbs past 20 seconds and the server log shows preemptions. Someone suggests the vector database is the bottleneck.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"vLLM startup log, then metrics and a warning during the slow period", text:"INFO 10-26 09:00:12 Model loading took 14.99 GiB and 6.21 seconds\nINFO 10-26 09:00:19 GPU KV cache size: 37,712 tokens\nINFO 10-26 09:00:19 Maximum concurrency for 32,768 tokens per request: 1.15x\nINFO 10-26 09:00:19 args: gpu_memory_utilization=0.90, max_model_len=32768, max_num_seqs=256, enable_prefix_caching=False, kv_cache_dtype=auto\n...\nINFO 10-26 14:21:07 metrics: Avg prompt throughput: 5120.4 tokens/s, Avg generation throughput: 38.2 tokens/s, Running: 4 reqs, Swapped: 0 reqs, Pending: 11 reqs, GPU KV cache usage: 98.7%\nWARNING 10-26 14:21:09 scheduler: Sequence group 1187 is preempted by PreemptionMode.RECOMPUTE mode because there is not enough KV cache space. This can affect the end-to-end performance. Increase gpu_memory_utilization or tensor_parallel_size to provide more KV cache memory. total_num_cumulative_preemption=51"},
     {label:"RAG app request stats (last hour) and prompt template", text:"request  history_tok  chunks_tok  question_tok  prompt_tok  retrieval_s  ttft_s\nr-1182   6,410        5,880       42            12,332      0.17         21.4\nr-1183   310          5,912       35            6,257       0.18         14.8\nr-1190   9,870        6,020       51            15,941      0.16         26.9\nr-1194   2,205        5,840       38            8,083       0.19         17.2\nmedian prompt_tok 11,850    max_tokens (output cap) 2,048\n\nprompt template: SYSTEM (310 tok) + 12 retrieved chunks + full chat history + question"},
     {label:"nvidia-smi during the slow period", text:"+-----------------------------------------+------------------------+----------------------+\n| GPU  Name                 Persistence-M | Bus-Id          Disp.A | Volatile Uncorr. ECC |\n| Fan  Temp   Perf          Pwr:Usage/Cap |           Memory-Usage | GPU-Util  Compute M. |\n|=========================================+========================+======================|\n|   0  24GB GPU                       On  |   00000000:01:00.0 Off |                  Off |\n| 30%   66C    P2            281W /  350W |   22104MiB /  24564MiB |    100%      Default |\n+-----------------------------------------+------------------------+----------------------+"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix19q1", type:"mc", q:"How much KV cache does one token of context need for this model?", options:["32 KiB","64 KiB","128 KiB","256 KiB"], answer:2, why:"2 (K and V) × 32 layers × 8 KV heads × 128 dims × 2 bytes (FP16) = 131,072 bytes = 128 KiB per token. 37,712 tokens × 128 KiB ≈ 4.6 GiB, about what's left of 0.90 × 24 GB after 15 GiB of weights and the activation workspace."},
     {id:"ix19q2", type:"mc", q:"About how many median-size requests (about 11,850 prompt tokens, plus output) fit in the KV cache at once?", options:["About 3","About 12","About 30","About 1"], answer:0, why:"37,712 / (11,850 + a few hundred generated tokens) ≈ 3. With 10-15 users, the rest wait. That matches 'Running: 4 reqs, Pending: 11' at 98.7% KV usage, with one of the four a shorter prompt."},
     {id:"ix19q3", type:"mc", q:"Why do preemptions make TTFT so much worse?", options:["Preempted requests are dropped and the user has to resend","Preemption copies the KV cache to the CPU, which is slow","Preemption triggers a model reload","In RECOMPUTE mode a preempted sequence's KV blocks are freed. When it resumes, its whole prompt is prefilled again, so long prompts get processed two or more times while others wait in the queue, wasting GPU compute exactly when it's scarcest"], answer:3, why:"'Swapped: 0' confirms nothing goes to the CPU. Recompute throws work away: a 12k-token prompt costs a full prefill every time it's re-admitted. Fifty-one preemptions means a lot of repeated prefill."},
     {id:"ix19q4", type:"mc", q:"Which change gives the biggest capacity gain on the same GPU without hurting answers much?", options:["Add more uvicorn workers to the RAG app","Set a token budget for prompts: a short summary plus the last few turns of history, the top 4 reranked chunks instead of 12, and the template reordered to SYSTEM + history + chunks + question with prefix caching on. At about 4k tokens per prompt, about 9 requests fit instead of 3, and an FP8 KV cache could double that again","Raise max_num_seqs to 512","Move the vector database to a faster server"], answer:1, why:"Capacity in sequences is KV size ÷ tokens per sequence, so cutting prompt length is the strongest lever. Putting history before the per-question chunks lets each turn reuse the previous turn's cached prefix. More app workers or a higher max_num_seqs just add queueing or preemption, and retrieval takes 0.17 s."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Computes KV bytes per token (2 × layers × KV heads × head_dim × bytes = 128 KiB) and the capacity (about 37.7k tokens ≈ 3 concurrent 12k-token requests), and links it to 'Running: 4, Pending: 11' at 98.7% KV usage.","Explains the latency mechanisms: queueing for KV space, RECOMPUTE preemption re-prefilling long prompts, and memory-bandwidth-bound decoding that slows as each sequence's KV grows.","Dismisses the vector DB (0.17 s retrieval vs 20 s+ TTFT), and treats 100% GPU utilization and full memory as non-diagnostic (vLLM pre-allocates).","Proposes a prompt token budget (summarized or windowed history, fewer reranked chunks), a cache-friendly template with prefix caching, and memory levers (FP8 KV cache, quantized weights, a lower max_model_len), with the expected capacity effect worked out.","Verifies with a replayed load test (KV usage under about 90%, preemptions near 0, p95 TTFT on target) and an answer-quality eval on a fixed question set, since trimming context can hurt answers."],
     reference:"The GPU is out of KV-cache memory, not compute, and the vector DB is innocent: retrieval takes 0.17 s of a 20 s+ TTFT. Each token of context stores K and V in every layer: 2 × 32 × 8 × 128 × 2 bytes = 128 KiB. After 15 GiB of weights, vLLM has room for 37,712 tokens (about 4.6 GiB). The median prompt is about 11,850 tokens (12 chunks plus the whole chat history), so only about three requests fit at once. The metrics agree: KV usage 98.7%, 4 running, 11 pending. Everyone else queues. When a running sequence needs more blocks, vLLM preempts another in RECOMPUTE mode and frees its KV. When that sequence resumes, its whole prompt is prefilled again, and 51 preemptions means the GPU keeps redoing work. Decoding slows too, since each new token reads the sequence's whole KV cache from memory. The template also defeats prefix caching, which is off anyway: chunks come before history, so prompts share only the system prompt. Fix the token budget first. Summarize old turns and keep the last few, send the top 4 reranked chunks, and order the template SYSTEM + history + chunks + question with prefix caching on. At about 4k tokens per prompt, about 9 requests fit, and kv_cache_dtype=fp8 roughly doubles that. Verify by replaying an hour of traffic: KV usage under 90%, preemptions near zero, p95 TTFT on target, and a fixed set of manual questions still answered correctly.",
     alternatives:["Quantize the weights (for example AWQ 4-bit, about 5 GiB instead of 15 GiB), which frees about 10 GiB for KV cache, roughly 80k more tokens, at a small quality cost you have to measure on your eval set.","Add admission control in the RAG app: cap concurrent requests at what the KV cache can hold, show a 'busy' message with queue position, and set max_model_len to 16k so one huge conversation can't push everyone else out."] },
   hints:["Work out how much memory one token of context costs in this model, then how many of today's prompts fit in 37,712 tokens.","Read the metrics line (running vs pending, KV usage) and the preemption warning. What happens to a preempted request in RECOMPUTE mode?","At 128 KiB per token, only about 3 prompts of 12k tokens fit. Cut the prompt (summarized history, top-4 reranked chunks), make the prefix cacheable, and consider an FP8 KV cache."] },

 { id:"ix20", title:"Consumer lag after adding partitions", level:5, domains:["p36","p37","p19","p13"],
   context:"The meter-readings Kafka topic feeds the billing-agg consumer group, a Kubernetes Deployment of 6 replicas that upserts running usage totals into PostgreSQL. Last Tuesday someone increased the topic from 6 to 12 partitions 'to add capacity'. Since then total lag has grown every day, two utility co-ops have reported customers' usage totals briefly going backwards, and giving the consumers more CPU didn't help.",
   evidence:[
     {label:"kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe --group billing-agg (excerpt, 14:00 today)", text:"GROUP        TOPIC           PARTITION  CURRENT-OFFSET  LOG-END-OFFSET  LAG       CONSUMER-ID           HOST          CLIENT-ID\nbilling-agg  meter-readings  0          881233010       881233102       92        consumer-1-7c2e9a...  /10.244.1.17  consumer-1\nbilling-agg  meter-readings  1          879012700       879012761       61        consumer-1-7c2e9a...  /10.244.1.17  consumer-1\nbilling-agg  meter-readings  2          864551020       864551088       68        consumer-2-0b81f4...  /10.244.2.9   consumer-2\n...\nbilling-agg  meter-readings  6          152524545       163388160       10863615  consumer-4-19ab3c...  /10.244.3.22  consumer-4\nbilling-agg  meter-readings  7          144715508       155347200       10631692  consumer-4-19ab3c...  /10.244.3.22  consumer-4\nbilling-agg  meter-readings  8          16071813        16071840        27        consumer-5-77d0e2...  /10.244.4.11  consumer-5\n...\nbilling-agg  meter-readings  11         15882381        15882410        29        consumer-6-a3c915...  /10.244.5.30  consumer-6"},
     {label:"Per-partition produce rate and top key (last 24 h)", text:"partition  avg msg/s  peak msg/s  top key (share)\n0          58         121         meter-11872 (0.4%)\n1          55         117         meter-50211 (0.4%)\n...\n6          305        640         coop-03 (81%)\n7          290        611         coop-11 (79%)\n8          31         66          meter-77310 (0.5%)\n...\nKeys: co-op ID for bulk feeds (coop-03, coop-11), meter ID for single meters.\nProducer partitioner: default (murmur2(key) % partition_count)"},
     {label:"Consumer config, processing loop and autoscaler", text:"# consumer.properties\npartition.assignment.strategy=org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.RangeAssignor\nenable.auto.commit=false\nmax.poll.records=500\n\n# processing loop (one thread per consumer)\nfor record in consumer.poll():\n    db.execute(UPSERT_TOTAL, (record.meter_id, record.reading_kwh, record.reading_time))   # ~1.8 ms round trip\nconsumer.commit()\n\nUPSERT_TOTAL = INSERT INTO usage_totals (meter_id, kwh, reading_time) VALUES (%s, %s, %s)\n               ON CONFLICT (meter_id) DO UPDATE SET kwh = EXCLUDED.kwh, reading_time = EXCLUDED.reading_time\n\n# hpa/billing-agg.yaml\nminReplicas: 6   maxReplicas: 6   (CPU target 70%)\nkubectl top: consumer-4 pod 38% CPU, other pods 6-11%"},
     {label:"Change record and broker health", text:"CHG-3307 (Tue 20 Oct 10:12): kafka-topics.sh --alter --topic meter-readings --partitions 12\n  Producers were not paused; consumer group rebalanced at 10:13.\n\nkafka-2 gc.log:\n[2026-10-26T13:58:40.811+0000][info][gc] GC(18231) Pause Young (Normal) (G1 Evacuation Pause) 1843M->612M(4096M) 412.338ms\n\nUnderReplicatedPartitions (all brokers): 0"}
   ],
   questions:[
     {id:"ix20q1", type:"mc", q:"Why is the lag concentrated where it is?", options:["The brokers are too slow","The two hottest keys (the coop-03 and coop-11 bulk feeds) now hash to partitions 6 and 7, and RangeAssignor gives each consumer contiguous partitions, so consumer-4 owns both. One single-threaded consumer can do about 550 msg/s, but those two partitions average about 595 msg/s","Partitions 6 and 7 are under-replicated","Consumer-4's pod has less CPU than the others"], answer:1, why:"RangeAssignor with 12 partitions and 6 consumers hands out pairs (0-1, 2-3, 4-5, 6-7...). Other consumers' partitions carry about 30-60 msg/s each and sit near zero lag. 1 / 1.8 ms ≈ 555 records/s, so about 40 msg/s of excess arrives on average, around 3.5M a day, which matches about 21.5M of lag after six days."},
     {id:"ix20q2", type:"mc", q:"Why did some usage totals go backwards?", options:["Adding partitions changed murmur2(key) % N, so new readings for a key went to a new partition while older readings were still waiting in the old partition's backlog. Kafka orders only within a partition, and the last-write-wins upsert applied an older cumulative reading after a newer one","PostgreSQL lost writes","Retention deleted unread messages","Consumers re-read messages after a rebalance and the duplicates added up"], answer:0, why:"When the count went from 6 to 12, coop-03's key moved from partition 0 to 6 (h mod 6 = 0 means h mod 12 is 0 or 6). For a while the same meters' readings sat in two partitions consumed independently. kwh = EXCLUDED.kwh has no guard on reading_time. The upsert sets the value rather than adding, so duplicates can't add up, but an out-of-order replay can regress it."},
     {id:"ix20q3", type:"mc", q:"Why didn't more CPU help?", options:["The JVM ignores extra CPU","Kafka limits each consumer to one core","The consumer is latency-bound: one thread does one synchronous ~1.8 ms upsert per record, so it tops out around 550 records/s whatever the CPU (consumer-4 sits at 38%)","The HPA removed the extra CPU"], answer:2, why:"Throughput here is 1 / (time per record), and the time is mostly waiting on a database round trip, not computing. Batching many rows per round trip, or processing in parallel while keeping per-key order, raises throughput. CPU doesn't."},
     {id:"ix20q4", type:"mc", q:"Which plan fixes throughput and correctness together?", options:["Increase to 48 partitions","Set auto.offset.reset=latest and restart to skip the backlog","Increase retention so nothing is lost while it catches up","Spread the hot partitions (RoundRobinAssignor, or maxReplicas 12 so each partition gets its own consumer), batch the upserts (for example 500 rows per transaction), make the upsert monotonic (update only if the incoming reading_time is newer), and plan any future partition changes deliberately"], answer:3, why:"More partitions don't help when the work is one hot key. auto.offset.reset only applies when there is no committed offset, and skipping data would corrupt billing anyway. Retention doesn't speed anything up. The plan in the last option addresses placement, per-record cost and ordering together."}
   ],
   task:{ prompt:"Submit your diagnosis, the tests that distinguish the hypotheses, the fix, and how you would verify it.",
     rubric:["Reads the group description: the lag sits on partitions 6 and 7, both owned by consumer-4, and ties that to key skew (the coop-03 and coop-11 bulk feeds) plus RangeAssignor's contiguous assignment.","Quantifies it: about 595 msg/s average into consumer-4 against about 550 msg/s capacity (1 / 1.8 ms), consistent with about 3.5M a day of lag growth, and explains why CPU didn't help (latency-bound, 38% CPU).","Explains the regressions: changing the partition count remaps keys (hash mod N), ordering holds only within a partition, and a last-write-wins upsert of cumulative readings goes backwards when applied out of order (also possible on at-least-once redelivery).","Fixes both throughput and correctness: rebalance (RoundRobin, or as many consumers as partitions), batch or pipeline the DB writes while keeping per-key order, a monotonic idempotent upsert, and lag-based autoscaling capped at the partition count. Notes that adding partitions doesn't add consumer capacity by itself.","Verifies (per-partition lag falls to steady state, even assignment in --describe, an audit query finds no decreasing totals, a lag alert) and dismisses the GC pause and broker health."],
     reference:"The new partitions didn't add capacity, because the bottleneck is one consumer. The group description puts almost all the lag, about 21.5M messages, on partitions 6 and 7, and RangeAssignor gave both to consumer-4. Those partitions carry the co-op bulk feeds. coop-03 and coop-11 are single keys that make up about 80% of their partitions' traffic, and after the change murmur2(key) % 12 put them in neighboring partitions. consumer-4 processes records one at a time with a synchronous 1.8 ms upsert, about 550 records/s at most, while partitions 6 and 7 average about 595 msg/s. Lag grows about 40 msg/s, roughly 3.5M a day. More CPU can't speed up a loop waiting on database round trips (38% CPU). The backwards totals are an ordering problem. Kafka orders only within a partition. When the count went from 6 to 12, coop-03 moved from partition 0 to 6 while producers kept running, so newer readings were consumed from 6 while older ones still sat in 0. The upsert is last-write-wins (kwh = EXCLUDED.kwh), so an older reading overwrote a newer one. A single GC pause and zero under-replicated partitions clear the brokers. Fix: switch to RoundRobinAssignor or run 12 consumers, batch the upserts (500 rows per transaction), and make the upsert monotonic (WHERE EXCLUDED.reading_time > usage_totals.reading_time). Autoscale on lag, capped at the partition count. Plan future partition changes: drain first, or migrate to a new topic. Verify that lag falls, assignment is even, and an audit query finds no decreasing totals.",
     alternatives:["Keep 6 consumers, but process each partition's records with bounded concurrency keyed by meter_id: per-key order is preserved while different meters run in parallel, which raises per-consumer throughput regardless of the assignor.","Re-key the co-op bulk feeds by meter ID instead of co-op ID, so one co-op's traffic spreads across partitions while per-meter ordering, which is all the totals need, is kept."] },
   hints:["In the group description, which partitions hold the lag, and which consumer owns them?","Compare the incoming rate on those partitions with what one single-threaded consumer doing ~1.8 ms upserts can process. Then ask what hash(key) % N does when N changes.","The hot keys moved to partitions 6 and 7, RangeAssignor gave both to consumer-4, and key remapping broke per-key ordering. Spread the partitions, batch the writes and make the upsert monotonic."] }
];
